export const DOCS_REF = "v0.13.3";
export const TRIGGERS_MD: string = "# Trigger reference\n\nEvery trigger condition and action in StarCraft and Brood War: what it does in the game,\nthe arguments it takes, how it is written as text, and where each argument is stored in\nthe map file. The game's behaviour described here is StarCraft 1.16.1's, found by running\ntest maps. Much of StarCraft: Remastered's trigger code has been read as well, and the\nparts a map maker would notice were checked by playing test maps. What it does differently\nis under [In StarCraft: Remastered](#in-starcraft-remastered).\n\nEach condition and action has a number, a line in the text form, and a table of where its\narguments are stored. These come from the same tables the editor reads and writes maps\nwith. The text form is SCMDraft 2's TrigEdit syntax, so an example line can be pasted into\nSCMDraft or into the editor's Text Trigger Editor.\n\nIn scmJS, triggers are edited in:\n\n- the **Trigger Editor** (Triggers ▸ Trigger Editor…, Ctrl+T), a form like StarEdit's;\n- the **Text Trigger Editor** (Ctrl+Shift+T), the same triggers in the text form. It comes\n  with the TrigEdit plugin, which is installed but off until you turn it on in Plugins ▸\n  Manage Plugins…. Triggers ▸ Import Triggers… and Export Triggers… use the same text\n  without it;\n- **TrigScript** (Triggers ▸ TrigScript…), triggers written as code. Each condition and\n  action is a function named after it, such as `setDeaths`; see the [TrigScript\n  guide](trigscript.md);\n- the **Mission Briefing Editor** (Triggers ▸ Mission Briefing Editor…), for\n  [briefings](#mission-briefing-actions).\n\nThe Trigger Map plugin, from Plugins ▸ Browse Plugins…, draws which triggers set and read\neach switch, death count and location, and lists triggers that can never run. See\n[Triggers](guide.md#triggers) in the user guide.\n\n## How triggers run\n\nA trigger is a list of up to 16 conditions and up to 64 actions, and the players it runs\nfor. When every condition holds, the game carries out the actions in order. A trigger that\nshould run straight away has [Always](#always) as its condition; a trigger with no\nconditions at all runs straight away too.\n\n![When the game goes through the triggers: every 31 frames, every 2 with hyper triggers](images/trig-cycle.svg)\n\n### The trigger cycle\n\nThe game does not check triggers continuously. It goes through them in *passes*, one every\n31 game frames: 1.3 seconds at Fastest, 2.1 seconds at Normal. The first pass is on the\ngame's second frame, so an Always trigger runs at once. A pass comes after that frame's\nunits have moved, fought and died, so a unit killed in a frame already counts in that\nframe's pass.\n\nIn a pass the game takes the players in the game in order, Player 1 to Player 8, and for\neach goes through that player's triggers top to bottom, in the order of the list. It asks\na trigger's conditions in order and stops at the first false one, so the conditions after\nit are not even asked. If they all hold, it carries out the actions in order. What one\ntrigger does is seen by every trigger after it in the same pass, with one exception for\ncounts of units (see [Counting units](#counting-units)).\n\nTwo things bring the next pass forward to the very next frame, for every player: a\n[Wait](#wait) running out, and the [countdown timer](#countdown-timer) reaching 0.\n\nGame speed does not change the 31-frame cycle or game time: a game second is 16 frames at\nevery speed, for [Elapsed Time](#elapsed-time) and the countdown alike. It changes only\nhow long a Wait lasts ([Waits](#waits)).\n\n### Hyper triggers\n\nA Wait of 0 runs out on the frame after it starts, and the pass it brings forward comes\nthe frame after that. So the fastest the game goes through the triggers is once every **2\nframes**. It never manages every frame.\n\n*Hyper triggers* use this: two or three preserved triggers of 62 or 63 Wait(0) actions\neach, owned by one player. Each pass starts another of their Waits, so the passes keep\ncoming every 2 frames. A slow pass, 31 frames after the last, comes only when none of them\nhas a Wait to start, and with that many Waits it comes so rarely that it does not matter\nin practice.\n\nThe cycle is shared, so one player's hyper triggers speed up every player's triggers. A\ncomputer player can carry them. The waits must be one frame long or less: 42 milliseconds\nat Fastest.\n\n### Who a trigger runs for\n\nA trigger is owned by one or more of the [players and groups](#players-and-groups): a\nplayer, a force, or All Players. A trigger owned by a group runs separately for each\nplayer in it, as if each had a copy of their own. Its conditions are checked and its\nactions carried out once per player, and inside it **Current Player** means whichever\nplayer that copy is running for.\n\nThe copies are handed out when the game loads the map, and only to players in the game:\nhuman and computer players, and rescuable and neutral slots, which run their triggers like\nanyone else. Only these owners get copies:\n\n- Players 1 to 8;\n- Force 1 to Force 4;\n- All Players.\n\nA trigger owned only by anything else (Players 9 to 12, Current Player, Foes, Allies,\nNeutral Players, Non-Allied Victory Players, None or the Unused groups) is dropped when\nthe game loads and never runs. Other editors let you tick those owners, but the game\nignores them. scmJS's Trigger Editor offers only the owners above; when a map's trigger\nalready has another ticked, it is shown with a note so you can clear it.\n\nOnly Use Map Settings games read a map's triggers at all. Melee and the other game types\nrun triggers built into the game. (See [`TRIG`](chk-format.md#trig) in the CHK reference.)\n\nEach owner gets its own copies, so a player reached twice, as with both \"Player 1\" and\n\"Force 1\" ticked, has two copies of the trigger and runs it twice in every pass.\n\nA player who is defeated, or leaves, stops running triggers. In a single-player game a\nplayer who has won keeps running them until the victory screen stops the game.\n\n### Once, or every time\n\nA trigger runs once for each player it runs for and is then finished, unless it carries\n[Preserve Trigger](#preserve-trigger). Then, once its actions are done, the game asks its\nconditions again on the next pass. A preserved trigger whose conditions stay true keeps\nfiring: one that adds a mineral adds one each pass. Preserve Trigger works wherever it\nstands in the list, even after a Wait: the game looks for it when the actions end.\n\nWhile a trigger is part way through its actions, held at a Wait, the game does not ask its\nconditions again. On each pass it carries on from the action it stopped at.\n\n### Waits\n\nEach player has one *wait slot*. A [Wait](#wait) takes it and holds the rest of its\ntrigger until its time has passed. While the slot is taken, another trigger of that player\nthat reaches a Wait stops there without starting its time, and tries again on each pass\nuntil it finds the slot free. The player's triggers with no Wait in their way keep running\nevery pass, and every player's slot is their own. A [Transmission](#transmission) for a\nperson, and [Center View](#center-view) in a single-player game, take the same slot.\n\n![One wait slot per player](images/trig-wait.svg)\n\nA Wait's time is in milliseconds of the game's speed, close to real time, not game time.\nEach frame takes a fixed number of milliseconds at each speed, the Wait lasts its time in\nframes, rounded up and at least one, and the trigger goes on in the pass on the frame\nafter:\n\n| Speed | Milliseconds a frame | Wait(0) goes on after | Wait(1000) goes on after |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Fastest | 42 | 2 frames | 25 frames |\n| Faster | 48 | 2 frames | 22 frames |\n| Fast | 56 | 2 frames | 19 frames |\n| Normal | 67 | 2 frames | 16 frames |\n| Slow | 83 | 2 frames | 14 frames |\n| Slower | 111 | 2 frames | 11 frames |\n| Slowest | 167 | 2 frames | 7 frames |\n\nA Wait of 4,294,967,295 never runs out, and takes that player's wait slot for the rest of\nthe game.\n\nOn a map with hyper triggers, a Wait in another trigger below them in the list hardly ever\ngets the slot: it can start only in the one slow pass. A Wait above them takes the slot\nfirst, and while it runs no hyper Wait can start, so the passes fall back to every 31\nframes until it runs out. Check Map points this out.\n\n### Timers with a death counter\n\nA [death count](#deaths) of a unit type the map never uses makes a timer that needs no\nWait, so it never holds up the player's other triggers or the hyper triggers. One\npreserved trigger adds 1 to the count every pass, and other triggers act when it reaches a\nnumber:\n\n```text\nTrigger(\"Player 1\"){\nConditions:\n\tAlways();\n\nActions:\n\tSet Deaths(\"Player 1\", \"Cave (Unused)\", Add, 1);\n\tPreserve Trigger();\n}\n\nTrigger(\"Player 1\"){\nConditions:\n\tDeaths(\"Player 1\", \"Cave (Unused)\", At least, 120);\n\nActions:\n\tCreate Unit(\"Player 8\", \"Zerg Zergling\", 6, \"Spawn\");\n\tSet Deaths(\"Player 1\", \"Cave (Unused)\", Set To, 0);\n\tPreserve Trigger();\n}\n```\n\nThe count goes up once per pass, so its speed depends on how often the passes come: one\nevery 31 frames normally, or one every 2 frames with hyper triggers, which is about 12 a\nsecond at Fastest. With hyper triggers, the second trigger above sends a wave about every\nten seconds. Setting the count back to 0 starts the timer again. The trigger's owner has\nto be in the game, or neither trigger runs.\n\n### Single player and multiplayer\n\nThe game runs every player's triggers on every machine, and only what reaches the screen\nis limited to the player the trigger runs for: text, sounds, portraits, pings and the\nview. A few actions behave differently in a single-player game:\n\n| Action | In a single-player game | In a multiplayer game |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [Transmission](#transmission), for a person | holds the trigger until the portrait has finished talking | holds the trigger for its duration, in milliseconds of game speed |\n| [Center View](#center-view), for a person | scrolls the screen and holds the trigger until it gets there | jumps the screen at once and does not hold |\n| [Pause Game](#pause-game) and [Unpause Game](#unpause-game) | pause and unpause, for a person's trigger | nothing |\n| The Esc key | skips the person's running triggers through their Waits, transmissions, text and sounds, unless [Disable Debug Mode](#disable-debug-mode) protects them | nothing |\n| A player who gets a result | keeps their triggers | loses their whole trigger list at once, winners and losers alike |\n\nFor a computer, rescuable or neutral player, Transmission, Center View, Pause Game and the\nother actions that touch the screen do nothing at all, and do not hold the trigger.\n\n### Winning and losing\n\n[Victory](#victory), [Defeat](#defeat) and [Draw](#draw) give the player the trigger runs\nfor a result, and the game settles the results at the end of each pass:\n\n- When any player won or drew in the pass, every other player still in the game with no\n  result of their own is **defeated**.\n- When every player in the game won or drew in the same pass, they all get a draw.\n- The first result a player gets stays. A Defeat or Draw that comes after a Victory for\n  the same player in the same pass waits, and by the next pass the player has already won.\n\nSo in a single-player game, a Victory for the person ends the game for the computers too,\nand a Draw is a draw only when everyone draws.\n\n### Counting units\n\n[Command](#command) and [Bring](#bring), and the Most and Least conditions built on them,\ncount units in ways that are easy to trip over:\n\n![What Bring counts in a location](images/trig-bring.svg)\n\n- **At most counts differently from At least and Exactly.** At least and Exactly count\n  finished units. At most counts every unit that exists, finished or not: a building going\n  up, an egg or cocoon (as Men), a unit whose training has begun. So the standard melee\n  defeat rule, Command(Current Player, Buildings, At most, 0), does not defeat a player\n  whose only building is still being built.\n- **Units aboard** a transport or bunker count for Bring only with At least and Exactly,\n  at the place of what carries them, and so do the interceptors or scarabs in a carrier or\n  reaver and a power-up a worker carries. With At most, Bring does not count them.\n- **A unit is in a location** when its box touches it, on the levels the location covers.\n  Ground units count by the ground under them; air units by the air bits of the location's\n  elevation settings.\n- **Hallucinations never count**, in Command or Bring, and neither do turrets.\n- **The counts are taken once per pass.** The first Command of a pass counts every\n  player's units, and every later Command in that pass, for any player, reads the same\n  counts.\n\nSo a unit made by [Create Unit](#create-unit) or handed over by [Give Units to\nPlayer](#give-units-to-player) later in the pass is invisible to Command until the next\npass. These take a new count straight away:\n\n- [Kill Unit](#kill-unit) and [Kill Unit At Location](#kill-unit-at-location);\n- [Remove Unit](#remove-unit) and [Remove Unit At Location](#remove-unit-at-location);\n- [Move Unit](#move-unit) and [Move Location](#move-location);\n- a [Bring](#bring) condition.\n\nBring keeps a count of its own for one location, with the same blind spot. It counts again\nwhen another location is asked about, and after the same actions.\n\n### Deaths and kills\n\nA unit's death counts only when it dies in the game: shot, killed by a spell, or consumed.\nThen its owner's [death count](#deaths) for its type goes up by one, and the killer's\n[kills](#kill) and score, if the killer is another player still in the game. Killing your\nown units counts nothing as a kill; killing an ally's counts.\n\n[Kill Unit](#kill-unit), [Remove Unit](#remove-unit) and their At Location forms count\nnothing at all: no death, no kill, no score. Neither does a hallucination dying.\n\nThe unit classes are counted apart. Deaths of Men, Buildings and Factories read the game's\nown counts of men, buildings and factories lost, which go up with each real death; Deaths\nof Any unit reads men and buildings lost together. They are not the sum of the per-type\ncounts: [Set Deaths](#set-deaths) on a Marine does not change Deaths of Men, while Set\nDeaths of Men changes the lost count itself.\n\n### Disabled conditions and actions\n\nA condition or action can be disabled without deleting it (a flag in its record). The game\nskips it: a disabled condition does not count, and a disabled action is not carried out.\nIn text, a disabled line starts with `;`.\n\n### Condition and action numbers the game does not have\n\nConditions are numbered up to 23, actions up to 59 and briefing actions up to 9. StarCraft\n1.16.1 does not check the number: when a trigger reaches a condition or action past the\nend, the game crashes. This was seen for a condition; for actions and briefing actions it\nis read from the game's code. Remastered checks: an unknown condition counts as false, so\nthe trigger does not run, and an unknown action is skipped. The editor keeps such a record\nas it is, and Check Map reports it.\n\n### In StarCraft: Remastered\n\nRemastered's own code for running triggers has been read: how it goes through a trigger's\nconditions and actions, the trigger flag 0x02, how it settles results at the end of a\npass, Victory, Defeat and Draw, and the conditions and actions named below. Those work as\nin 1.16.1, with these differences:\n\n- An unknown condition or action number does not crash it (see above).\n- A [Map Revealer](#create-unit) made by Create Unit stays. 1.16.1 creates it and removes\n  it straight away as unplaceable. (Found playing Remastered.)\n- [Play WAV](#play-wav) plays Ogg Vorbis sounds as well as WAV, in a Brood War map as in a\n  Remastered one. 1.16.1 plays only WAV. (Found playing Remastered.)\n- A map whose triggers use [EUD](#eud) values makes Remastered run its triggers in an EUD\n  mode, where Deaths conditions and Set Deaths actions reach the game's memory.\n\nThese were read in its code and also played, with the same result as in 1.16.1: Create\nUnit makes nothing for Player 12, Set Deaths for Player 10 writes nothing in a map without\nEUD triggers, and Bring finds nothing in a location stored inverted.\n\nTwo parts were not read: how Remastered hands out the copies of the triggers when the map\nloads, which is in a scrambled part of its code, and the timing of the passes. For those,\nand for the actions not named here, the page describes 1.16.1.\n\n## Conditions\n\nA condition is a test a trigger makes on every pass, unless the trigger is part way\nthrough its actions. These are all of them:\n\n| Condition | True when |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Accumulate](#accumulate) | a player has a given amount of ore or gas |\n| [Always](#always) | always |\n| [Bring](#bring) | a player has a given number of units in a location |\n| [Command](#command) | a player has a given number of units anywhere |\n| [Command the Least](#command-the-least) | the player has the fewest of a unit type |\n| [Command the Least At](#command-the-least-at) | the player has the fewest of a unit type in a location |\n| [Command the Most](#command-the-most) | the player has the most of a unit type |\n| [Command the Most At](#command-the-most-at) | the player has the most of a unit type in a location |\n| [Countdown Timer](#countdown-timer) | the countdown timer shows a given time |\n| [Deaths](#deaths) | a player's death count for a unit type is a given number |\n| [Elapsed Time](#elapsed-time) | a given time has passed since the game started |\n| [Highest Score](#highest-score) | the player has the highest score of a kind |\n| [Kill](#kill) | a player has killed a given number of a unit type |\n| [Least Kills](#least-kills) | the player has killed the fewest of a unit type |\n| [Least Resources](#least-resources) | the player has the least ore or gas |\n| [Lowest Score](#lowest-score) | the player has the lowest score of a kind |\n| [Mission Briefing](#mission-briefing) | used by briefings only |\n| [Most Kills](#most-kills) | the player has killed the most of a unit type |\n| [Most Resources](#most-resources) | the player has the most ore or gas |\n| [Never](#never) | never |\n| [Opponents](#opponents) | a player has a given number of opponents left |\n| [Score](#score) | a player's score of a kind is a given number |\n| [Switch](#switch) | a switch is set, or clear |\n\nConditions that compare a number take a [comparison](#comparisons): At least, At most or\nExactly. All of a trigger's conditions must hold for it to run.\n\n### Accumulate\n\nTrue when the player has at least, at most or exactly the amount of ore, gas, or ore and\ngas.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 4 -->\n**Condition 4** · `Accumulate(Player, Comparison, Amount, Resource);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 4–7 |\n| Comparison | [At least, At most or Exactly](#comparisons) | byte 14 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 8–11 |\n| Resource | [ore, gas, or ore and gas](#resources) | byte 16 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nAccumulate(\"Current Player\", At least, 500, ore);\n```\n\n### Always\n\nAlways true. A trigger that should run as soon as the game starts, or on every cycle with\n[Preserve Trigger](#preserve-trigger), has this as its condition. A trigger with no\nconditions at all behaves the same.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 22 -->\n**Condition 22** · `Always();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nAlways();\n```\n\n### Bring\n\nTrue when the player has at least, at most or exactly the number of units of the type\ninside the location. A location's elevation settings (which of low, middle and high ground\nand air it covers) decide which units it counts.\n\nA unit counts when its box touches the location. With At least or Exactly, Bring counts\nfinished units and the units aboard a transport in the location; with At most, it counts\nevery unit there, finished or not, but nothing aboard. See [Counting\nunits](#counting-units).\n\nA location stored inverted, its right edge left of its left edge or its bottom above its\ntop, holds nothing for Bring: the condition finds no units in it, so At least 1 is never\ntrue. (Run in 1.16.1 and found playing Remastered.) Create Unit uses only a location's\nmiddle, which is the same point for an inverted location.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 3 -->\n**Condition 3** · `Bring(Player, Unit, Location, Comparison, Amount);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 4–7 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 12–13 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n| Comparison | [At least, At most or Exactly](#comparisons) | byte 14 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 8–11 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nBring(\"Current Player\", \"Terran Marine\", \"Beacon\", At least, 1);\n```\n\n### Command\n\nTrue when the player has at least, at most or exactly the number of units of the type\nanywhere on the map. With At least or Exactly it counts finished units; with At most,\nevery unit, including buildings still going up and eggs. The counts are taken once in each\npass, so a unit created later in the pass is not seen until the next one. See [Counting\nunits](#counting-units).\n\nThe example is the standard melee defeat rule, which every melee game runs: a player who\ncommands no buildings loses.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 2 -->\n**Condition 2** · `Command(Player, Unit, Comparison, Amount);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 4–7 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 12–13 |\n| Comparison | [At least, At most or Exactly](#comparisons) | byte 14 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 8–11 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nCommand(\"Current Player\", \"Buildings\", At most, 0);\n```\n\n### Command the Least\n\nTrue when the player the trigger runs for has fewer units of the type than any other\nplayer. It counts finished units.\n\nA tie counts: when every player has 0, the condition is true for all of them. The game\ncompares the player with every player in the game, rescuable and neutral slots included.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 16 -->\n**Condition 16** · `Command the Least(Unit);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 12–13 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nCommand the Least(\"Buildings\");\n```\n\n### Command the Least At\n\nAs [Command the Least](#command-the-least), counting only the units inside the location.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 17 -->\n**Condition 17** · `Command the Least At(Unit, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 12–13 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nCommand the Least At(\"Men\", \"Arena\");\n```\n\n### Command the Most\n\nTrue when the player the trigger runs for has more units of the type than any other\nplayer. It counts finished units.\n\nA tie counts: when every player has 0, the condition is true for all of them. The game\ncompares the player with every player in the game, rescuable and neutral slots included.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 6 -->\n**Condition 6** · `Command the Most(Unit);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 12–13 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nCommand the Most(\"Buildings\");\n```\n\n### Command the Most At\n\nAs [Command the Most](#command-the-most), counting only the units inside the location. A\nking-of-the-hill map checks who holds the hill with it.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 7 -->\n**Condition 7** · `Command the Most At(Unit, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 12–13 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nCommand the Most At(\"Men\", \"Hill\");\n```\n\n### Countdown Timer\n\nTrue when the countdown timer shows at least, at most or exactly the number of seconds.\nThe timer is started and changed with [Set Countdown Timer](#set-countdown-timer).\n\n<!-- generated: condition 1 -->\n**Condition 1** · `Countdown Timer(Comparison, Amount);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Comparison | [At least, At most or Exactly](#comparisons) | byte 14 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 8–11 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nCountdown Timer(At most, 0);\n```\n\n### Deaths\n\nTrue when the number of units of the type the player has lost is at least, at most or\nexactly the amount. Only units that die in the game count: [Kill Unit](#kill-unit) and\n[Remove Unit](#remove-unit) add nothing. For Men, Buildings, Factories and Any unit it\nreads the game's own counts of units lost, not the sum of the types (see [Deaths and\nkills](#deaths-and-kills)).\n\nEvery player has a death count for every unit type, whether or not that unit is ever on\nthe map, and [Set Deaths](#set-deaths) can change it. That makes the death counts of unit\ntypes a map does not use the usual place to keep numbers: a counter, a timer, a state.\nEach is a whole number from 0 to 4,294,967,295.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 15 -->\n**Condition 15** · `Deaths(Player, Unit, Comparison, Amount);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 4–7 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 12–13 |\n| Comparison | [At least, At most or Exactly](#comparisons) | byte 14 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 8–11 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nDeaths(\"Current Player\", \"Cave (Unused)\", Exactly, 3);\n```\n\n### Elapsed Time\n\nTrue when at least, at most or exactly the number of game seconds have passed since the\ngame started. A game second is 16 frames at every game speed, and the clock stops while\nthe game is paused, by a menu or by [Pause Game](#pause-game).\n\n<!-- generated: condition 12 -->\n**Condition 12** · `Elapsed Time(Comparison, Amount);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Comparison | [At least, At most or Exactly](#comparisons) | byte 14 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 8–11 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nElapsed Time(At least, 600);\n```\n\n### Highest Score\n\nTrue when the player the trigger runs for has the highest score of the [kind](#scores).\n\nA tie counts: when every player has 0, the condition is true for all of them. The game\ncompares the player with every player in the game, rescuable and neutral slots included.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 9 -->\n**Condition 9** · `Highest Score(Score);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Score | a [score type](#scores) | byte 16 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nHighest Score(Kills);\n```\n\n### Kill\n\nTrue when the player has killed at least, at most or exactly the number of units of the\ntype. A player's own units never count as kills; an ally's do. Units killed by triggers\nare nobody's kills.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 5 -->\n**Condition 5** · `Kill(Player, Unit, Comparison, Amount);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 4–7 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 12–13 |\n| Comparison | [At least, At most or Exactly](#comparisons) | byte 14 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 8–11 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nKill(\"Current Player\", \"Zerg Zergling\", At least, 20);\n```\n\n### Least Kills\n\nTrue when the player the trigger runs for has killed fewer units of the type than any\nother player.\n\nA tie counts: when every player has 0, the condition is true for all of them. The game\ncompares the player with every player in the game, rescuable and neutral slots included.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 18 -->\n**Condition 18** · `Least Kills(Unit);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 12–13 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeast Kills(\"Men\");\n```\n\n### Least Resources\n\nTrue when the player the trigger runs for has less of the resource than any other player.\n\nA tie counts: when every player has 0, the condition is true for all of them. The game\ncompares the player with every player in the game, rescuable and neutral slots included.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 20 -->\n**Condition 20** · `Least Resources(Resource);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Resource | [ore, gas, or ore and gas](#resources) | byte 16 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeast Resources(ore);\n```\n\n### Lowest Score\n\nTrue when the player the trigger runs for has the lowest score of the [kind](#scores).\n\nA tie counts: when every player has 0, the condition is true for all of them. The game\ncompares the player with every player in the game, rescuable and neutral slots included.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 19 -->\n**Condition 19** · `Lowest Score(Score);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Score | a [score type](#scores) | byte 16 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLowest Score(Total);\n```\n\n### Mission Briefing\n\nThe condition of every trigger in a mission briefing. In a map's own triggers it is the\nsame as [Never](#never). See [Mission briefing actions](#mission-briefing-actions).\n\n<!-- generated: condition 13 -->\n**Condition 13** · `Mission Briefing();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Most Kills\n\nTrue when the player the trigger runs for has killed more units of the type than any other\nplayer.\n\nA tie counts: when every player has 0, the condition is true for all of them. The game\ncompares the player with every player in the game, rescuable and neutral slots included.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 8 -->\n**Condition 8** · `Most Kills(Unit);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 12–13 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nMost Kills(\"Men\");\n```\n\n### Most Resources\n\nTrue when the player the trigger runs for has more of the resource than any other player.\n\nA tie counts: when every player has 0, the condition is true for all of them. The game\ncompares the player with every player in the game, rescuable and neutral slots included.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 10 -->\n**Condition 10** · `Most Resources(Resource);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Resource | [ore, gas, or ore and gas](#resources) | byte 16 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nMost Resources(ore and gas);\n```\n\n### Never\n\nNever true. A trigger with it never runs; the actions are kept, which is a way to keep a\ntrigger in the list switched off.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 23 -->\n**Condition 23** · `Never();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nNever();\n```\n\n### Opponents\n\nTrue when the player has at least, at most or exactly the number of opponents still in the\ngame. The player's *side* is the player and every player allied for victory with it both\nways; an opponent is a player in the game who is not allied for victory, both ways, with\neveryone on that side. Rescuable and neutral slots are nobody's opponents, and a defeated\nplayer is no longer one.\n\nThe game's own melee rules use it: a player with no opponents left wins, as in the example\nbelow. Blizzard's melee maps carry the same rule in another form, Command(Non Allied\nVictory Players, Buildings, At most, 0), which runs only when such a map is played as Use\nMap Settings.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 14 -->\n**Condition 14** · `Opponents(Player, Comparison, Amount);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 4–7 |\n| Comparison | [At least, At most or Exactly](#comparisons) | byte 14 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 8–11 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nOpponents(\"Current Player\", At most, 0);\n```\n\n### Score\n\nTrue when the player's score of the [kind](#scores) is at least, at most or exactly the\namount.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 21 -->\n**Condition 21** · `Score(Player, Score, Comparison, Amount);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 4–7 |\n| Score | a [score type](#scores) | byte 16 |\n| Comparison | [At least, At most or Exactly](#comparisons) | byte 14 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 8–11 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nScore(\"Current Player\", Custom, At least, 100);\n```\n\n### Switch\n\nTrue when the [switch](#switches) is set, or when it is clear.\n\n<!-- generated: condition 11 -->\n**Condition 11** · `Switch(Switch, State);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the condition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Switch | one of the 256 [switches](#switches) | byte 16 |\n| State | [set or not set](#switches) | byte 14 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nSwitch(\"Switch 1\", set);\n```\n\n## Actions\n\nAn action is what a trigger does once its conditions hold. These are all of them:\n\n| Action | Does |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Center View](#center-view) | moves the player's screen to a location |\n| [Comment](#comment) | nothing in the game; names the trigger in an editor |\n| [Create Unit](#create-unit) | creates units at a location |\n| [Create Unit with Properties](#create-unit-with-properties) | creates units with hit points, energy and other properties set |\n| [Defeat](#defeat) | the player loses |\n| [Display Text Message](#display-text-message) | shows text on the player's screen |\n| [Draw](#draw) | the player draws |\n| [Give Units to Player](#give-units-to-player) | hands units at a location to another player |\n| [Kill Unit](#kill-unit) | kills every unit of a type a player owns |\n| [Kill Unit At Location](#kill-unit-at-location) | kills units in a location |\n| [Leader Board Control](#leader-board-control) | a leaderboard of units owned |\n| [Leader Board Control At Location](#leader-board-control-at-location) | a leaderboard of units owned in a location |\n| [Leader Board Greed](#leader-board-greed) | a leaderboard of ore and gas towards a goal |\n| [Leader Board Kills](#leader-board-kills) | a leaderboard of kills |\n| [Leader Board Points](#leader-board-points) | a leaderboard of a score |\n| [Leader Board Resources](#leader-board-resources) | a leaderboard of ore or gas |\n| [Leaderboard Computer Players](#leaderboard-computer-players) | whether computer players are on leaderboards |\n| [Leaderboard Goal Control](#leaderboard-goal-control) | a leaderboard of units owned, towards a goal |\n| [Leaderboard Goal Control At Location](#leaderboard-goal-control-at-location) | the same, in a location |\n| [Leaderboard Goal Kills](#leaderboard-goal-kills) | a leaderboard of kills, towards a goal |\n| [Leaderboard Goal Points](#leaderboard-goal-points) | a leaderboard of a score, towards a goal |\n| [Leaderboard Goal Resources](#leaderboard-goal-resources) | a leaderboard of ore or gas, towards a goal |\n| [Minimap Ping](#minimap-ping) | pings a location on the player's minimap |\n| [Modify Unit Energy](#modify-unit-energy) | sets units' energy to a percentage |\n| [Modify Unit Hanger Count](#modify-unit-hanger-count) | adds interceptors or scarabs |\n| [Modify Unit Hit Points](#modify-unit-hit-points) | sets units' hit points to a percentage |\n| [Modify Unit Resource Amount](#modify-unit-resource-amount) | sets how much a mineral field or geyser holds |\n| [Modify Unit Shield Points](#modify-unit-shield-points) | sets units' shields to a percentage |\n| [Move Location](#move-location) | centres a location on a unit |\n| [Move Unit](#move-unit) | moves units from one location to another instantly |\n| [Mute Unit Speech](#mute-unit-speech) | silences unit voices |\n| [Order](#order) | orders units to move, patrol or attack |\n| [Pause Game](#pause-game) | pauses the game, in single player |\n| [Pause Timer](#pause-timer) | stops the countdown timer |\n| [Play WAV](#play-wav) | plays a sound |\n| [Preserve Trigger](#preserve-trigger) | keeps the trigger so it can run again |\n| [Remove Unit](#remove-unit) | removes every unit of a type a player owns |\n| [Remove Unit At Location](#remove-unit-at-location) | removes units in a location |\n| [Run AI Script](#run-ai-script) | starts a computer player's AI script |\n| [Run AI Script At Location](#run-ai-script-at-location) | starts an AI script that uses a location |\n| [Set Alliance Status](#set-alliance-status) | makes the player an enemy or ally of another |\n| [Set Countdown Timer](#set-countdown-timer) | sets the countdown timer |\n| [Set Deaths](#set-deaths) | changes a death count |\n| [Set Doodad State](#set-doodad-state) | turns doors and traps on or off |\n| [Set Invincibility](#set-invincibility) | makes units invincible, or not |\n| [Set Mission Objectives](#set-mission-objectives) | sets the text of the objectives screen |\n| [Set Next Scenario](#set-next-scenario) | chooses the map that follows a win |\n| [Set Resources](#set-resources) | changes a player's ore or gas |\n| [Set Score](#set-score) | changes a player's score |\n| [Set Switch](#set-switch) | sets, clears, toggles or randomizes a switch |\n| [Talking Portrait](#talking-portrait) | shows a unit's portrait talking |\n| [Transmission](#transmission) | a message with a portrait, a sound and a minimap ping |\n| [Unmute Unit Speech](#unmute-unit-speech) | lets unit voices play again |\n| [Unpause Game](#unpause-game) | resumes a game Pause Game stopped |\n| [Unpause Timer](#unpause-timer) | restarts the countdown timer |\n| [Victory](#victory) | the player wins |\n| [Wait](#wait) | holds the rest of the trigger for a time |\n| [Disable Debug Mode](#disable-debug-mode) | stops the Esc key from skipping the trigger |\n| [Enable Debug Mode](#enable-debug-mode) | lets the Esc key skip the trigger again |\n\nActions are carried out in order, for the player the trigger is running for. Actions that\nact on \"the player\" — showing text, moving the screen, winning — act on that player.\n\n### Center View\n\nMoves the player's screen so the location is in the middle of it. In a single-player game\nthe screen scrolls there and the trigger is held, like a [Wait](#wait), until it arrives;\nin a multiplayer game it jumps at once. It does nothing for a computer player, while the\ngame is paused, or with no location.\n\n<!-- generated: action 10 -->\n**Action 10** · `Center View(Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nCenter View(\"Base\");\n```\n\n### Comment\n\nDoes nothing in the game. Editors show the text as the trigger's name in the trigger list,\nwhich is what it is for.\n\n<!-- generated: action 47 -->\n**Action 47** · `Comment(Text);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Text | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nComment(\"Wave 3 spawns\");\n```\n\n### Create Unit\n\nCreates the number of units of the type for the player, at the middle of the location. A\nunit that does not fit there is placed at the nearest spot it fits, up to 128 pixels away;\na unit with no room that close is not created. A building is not moved: it is not created\non ground it cannot stand on, or where something is in the way. Mineral fields and geysers\nare always created for Player 12. A count of 0 creates one unit, although the text form\nwrites it as All.\n\nNot every unit can be created:\n\n- **Only Players 1 to 8.** Create Unit makes nothing for Players 9 to 12 or None, and with\n  All Players it makes units only for Players 1 to 8. (Run in 1.16.1 and found playing\n  Remastered.)\n- **Not a class.** Any unit, Men, Buildings and Factories make nothing; it needs a unit\n  type.\n- **Only the types StarEdit's unit palette offers.** With the game's own unit data that is\n  168 of the 228 types. The rest make nothing: larvae, eggs and cocoons, the tank and\n  Goliath turrets, interceptors, scarabs, the nuclear missile, Scanner Sweep, Disruption\n  Web, Dark Swarm, the doors and traps, start locations, the race markers, the minerals\n  and gas a worker carries, the four heroes Arcturus Mengsk (Battlecruiser), Aldaris,\n  Raszagal and Gerard DuGalle, and the unused types. (Measured in 1.16.1; Remastered's\n  code makes the same check.)\n- **A Map Revealer** is created and at once removed as unplaceable in 1.16.1. Remastered\n  keeps it. (Found playing Remastered.)\n\nThe scmJS Trigger Editor's unit list for Create Unit shows only the types that can be\ncreated, once the game data is installed.\n\nThe action has a properties field, bytes 20–23, like [Create Unit with\nProperties](#create-unit-with-properties). The game reads it for Create Unit too: a value\nfrom 1 to 64 creates the units with that properties slot. Editors write 0 there.\n\n<!-- generated: action 44 -->\n**Action 44** · `Create Unit(Player, Unit, Count, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type a trigger can make; the [unit classes](#unit-classes) make nothing | bytes 24–25 |\n| Count | a number of units, 1 to 255 (0 makes one) | byte 27 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nCreate Unit(\"Player 8\", \"Zerg Zergling\", 6, \"Spawn\");\n```\n\n### Create Unit with Properties\n\nAs [Create Unit](#create-unit), and the new units take the properties of one of the map's\n64 *unit properties slots*: hit points, shields and energy as percentages, resources,\nhangar count, and states such as cloaked, burrowed, lifted or hallucinated. The slots are\nstored in the map's `UPRP` section; the editor's Triggers ▸ Unit Properties Slots… edits\nthem. A unit is created cloaked or burrowed only if its owner has the technology that lets\nit, and resources are kept to 16 bits. It can create the same units, for the same players,\nas Create Unit.\n\n<!-- generated: action 11 -->\n**Action 11** · `Create Unit with Properties(Player, Unit, Count, Location, Properties);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type a trigger can make; the [unit classes](#unit-classes) make nothing | bytes 24–25 |\n| Count | a number of units, 1 to 255 (0 makes one) | byte 27 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n| Properties | a [unit properties slot](#create-unit-with-properties) | bytes 20–23 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nCreate Unit with Properties(\"Player 8\", \"Zerg Hydralisk\", 4, \"Spawn\", 1);\n```\n\n### Defeat\n\nThe player loses. Nobody else is affected. In a Use Map Settings game the player's units\nstay on the map, idle, and the player stops running triggers and drops out of every group.\nA Defeat that comes after a [Victory](#victory) for the same player in the same pass\nwaits, and so never takes effect. See [Winning and losing](#winning-and-losing).\n\n<!-- generated: action 2 -->\n**Action 2** · `Defeat();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nDefeat();\n```\n\n### Display Text Message\n\nShows the text on the player's screen. *Always Display* shows it even to a player who has\nturned subtitles off; *Don't Always Display* follows that setting. The text can carry the\ngame's colour codes. Only the player the trigger runs for sees it: a computer player's\nDisplay Text shows on nobody's screen. The text stays for 4 seconds, or 50 milliseconds\nfor each character of a longer text, in real time.\n\n<!-- generated: action 9 -->\n**Action 9** · `Display Text Message(Display, Text);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Display | Always Display or Don't Always Display | bit 0x04 of byte 28 |\n| Text | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nDisplay Text Message(Always Display, \"Defend the base until help arrives.\");\n```\n\n### Draw\n\nGives the player a draw. It is a draw for everyone only when every player in the game\ndraws or wins in the same pass; otherwise every other player still in the game with no\nresult is defeated. See [Winning and losing](#winning-and-losing).\n\n<!-- generated: action 56 -->\n**Action 56** · `Draw();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nDraw();\n```\n\n### Give Units to Player\n\nHands the number of units of the type in the location from one player to another. With a\ngroup as the player, the count is for the whole group. A transport takes its cargo along\nto the new owner; a unit inside a transport is not given on its own.\n\n<!-- generated: action 48 -->\n**Action 48** · `Give Units to Player(From, To, Unit, Count, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| From | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| To | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 20–23 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Count | a number of units, 1 to 255, or All (stored as 0) | byte 27 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nGive Units to Player(\"Player 12\", \"Current Player\", \"Any unit\", All, \"Rescue\");\n```\n\n### Kill Unit\n\nKills every unit of the type the player owns, anywhere on the map, with their death\nanimation. A unit killed this way adds nothing to anyone's [death count](#deaths), kills\nor score: only a unit that dies in the game, shot or killed by a spell, does. A death\ncounter meant to see a unit die therefore never sees Kill Unit. *Any unit* means every\nunit the player owns, mineral fields included.\n\n<!-- generated: action 22 -->\n**Action 22** · `Kill Unit(Player, Unit);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nKill Unit(\"Player 8\", \"Zerg Larva\");\n```\n\n### Kill Unit At Location\n\nAs [Kill Unit](#kill-unit), for the number of units of the type in the location, units\nloaded in transports there included. With a group as the player, the count is for each\nplayer in it.\n\n<!-- generated: action 23 -->\n**Action 23** · `Kill Unit At Location(Player, Unit, Count, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Count | a number of units, 1 to 255, or All (stored as 0) | byte 27 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nKill Unit At Location(\"All Players\", \"Men\", All, \"Lava\");\n```\n\n### Leader Board Control\n\nShows a leaderboard of how many units of the type each player owns. A map shows one\nleaderboard at a time: showing another replaces it. It is one leaderboard for everyone,\nwhichever player's trigger shows it, and the last one shown wins.\n\n<!-- generated: action 17 -->\n**Action 17** · `Leader Board Control(Label, Unit);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Label | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeader Board Control(\"Marines\", \"Terran Marine\");\n```\n\n### Leader Board Control At Location\n\nA leaderboard of how many units of the type each player has in the location.\n\n<!-- generated: action 18 -->\n**Action 18** · `Leader Board Control At Location(Label, Unit, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Label | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeader Board Control At Location(\"On the hill\", \"Men\", \"Hill\");\n```\n\n### Leader Board Greed\n\nA leaderboard of each player's ore and gas together, towards the goal.\n\n<!-- generated: action 40 -->\n**Action 40** · `Leader Board Greed(Goal);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Goal | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeader Board Greed(5000);\n```\n\n### Leader Board Kills\n\nA leaderboard of how many units of the type each player has killed.\n\n<!-- generated: action 20 -->\n**Action 20** · `Leader Board Kills(Label, Unit);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Label | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeader Board Kills(\"Kills\", \"Men\");\n```\n\n### Leader Board Points\n\nA leaderboard of each player's score of the [kind](#scores). With Custom, the numbers are\nwhatever [Set Score](#set-score) has made them.\n\n<!-- generated: action 21 -->\n**Action 21** · `Leader Board Points(Label, Score);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Label | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Score | a [score type](#scores) | bytes 24–25 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeader Board Points(\"Points\", Custom);\n```\n\n### Leader Board Resources\n\nA leaderboard of each player's ore, gas, or ore and gas.\n\n<!-- generated: action 19 -->\n**Action 19** · `Leader Board Resources(Label, Resource);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Label | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Resource | [ore, gas, or ore and gas](#resources) | bytes 24–25 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeader Board Resources(\"Minerals\", ore);\n```\n\n### Leaderboard Computer Players\n\nWhether computer players appear on leaderboards: enable, disable or toggle.\n\n<!-- generated: action 32 -->\n**Action 32** · `Leaderboard Computer Players(State);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| State | [enable, disable or toggle](#states) | byte 27 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeaderboard Computer Players(disable);\n```\n\n### Leaderboard Goal Control\n\nAs [Leader Board Control](#leader-board-control), counting towards the goal.\n\n<!-- generated: action 33 -->\n**Action 33** · `Leaderboard Goal Control(Label, Unit, Goal);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Label | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Goal | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeaderboard Goal Control(\"Build 10\", \"Terran Marine\", 10);\n```\n\n### Leaderboard Goal Control At Location\n\nAs [Leader Board Control At Location](#leader-board-control-at-location), counting towards\nthe goal.\n\n<!-- generated: action 34 -->\n**Action 34** · `Leaderboard Goal Control At Location(Label, Unit, Goal, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Label | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Goal | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeaderboard Goal Control At Location(\"Hold the hill\", \"Men\", 5, \"Hill\");\n```\n\n### Leaderboard Goal Kills\n\nAs [Leader Board Kills](#leader-board-kills), counting towards the goal.\n\n<!-- generated: action 36 -->\n**Action 36** · `Leaderboard Goal Kills(Label, Unit, Goal);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Label | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Goal | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeaderboard Goal Kills(\"First to 50\", \"Men\", 50);\n```\n\n### Leaderboard Goal Points\n\nAs [Leader Board Points](#leader-board-points), counting towards the goal.\n\n<!-- generated: action 37 -->\n**Action 37** · `Leaderboard Goal Points(Label, Score, Goal);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Label | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Score | a [score type](#scores) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Goal | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeaderboard Goal Points(\"Points\", Custom, 100);\n```\n\n### Leaderboard Goal Resources\n\nAs [Leader Board Resources](#leader-board-resources), counting towards the goal.\n\n<!-- generated: action 35 -->\n**Action 35** · `Leaderboard Goal Resources(Label, Goal, Resource);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Label | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Goal | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n| Resource | [ore, gas, or ore and gas](#resources) | bytes 24–25 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nLeaderboard Goal Resources(\"Mine 1000\", 1000, ore);\n```\n\n### Minimap Ping\n\nPings the middle of the location on the player's minimap. Only the player the trigger runs\nfor sees it.\n\n<!-- generated: action 28 -->\n**Action 28** · `Minimap Ping(Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nMinimap Ping(\"Enemy base\");\n```\n\n### Modify Unit Energy\n\nSets the energy of the number of units of the type in the location to the percentage of\ntheir maximum. Only units that use energy are changed, and the count passes over the\nothers. Units inside a transport are not reached.\n\n<!-- generated: action 50 -->\n**Action 50** · `Modify Unit Energy(Player, Unit, Percent, Count, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Percent | a percentage, 0 to 100 | bytes 20–23 |\n| Count | a number of units, 1 to 255, or All (stored as 0) | byte 27 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nModify Unit Energy(\"Current Player\", \"Terran Ghost\", 100, All, \"Anywhere\");\n```\n\n### Modify Unit Hanger Count\n\nAdds the amount of interceptors to carriers, or scarabs to reavers, for the number of\nunits of the type in the location, up to the most each can hold. A unit that has no room\nfor more does not use up the count.\n\n<!-- generated: action 53 -->\n**Action 53** · `Modify Unit Hanger Count(Player, Unit, Amount, Count, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n| Count | a number of units, 1 to 255, or All (stored as 0) | byte 27 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nModify Unit Hanger Count(\"Current Player\", \"Protoss Carrier\", 8, All, \"Anywhere\");\n```\n\n### Modify Unit Hit Points\n\nSets the hit points of the number of units of the type in the location to the percentage\nof their maximum. A unit is never left below 1/256 of a hit point, so 0% does not kill it.\nUnits inside a transport are not reached.\n\n<!-- generated: action 49 -->\n**Action 49** · `Modify Unit Hit Points(Player, Unit, Percent, Count, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Percent | a percentage, 0 to 100 | bytes 20–23 |\n| Count | a number of units, 1 to 255, or All (stored as 0) | byte 27 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nModify Unit Hit Points(\"Player 8\", \"Men\", 50, All, \"Anywhere\");\n```\n\n### Modify Unit Resource Amount\n\nSets how much ore or gas the mineral fields and geysers of the player in the location\nhold. There is no unit argument: it applies to every resource the player owns there. The\namount is kept to 16 bits, so 100,000 becomes 34,464.\n\n<!-- generated: action 52 -->\n**Action 52** · `Modify Unit Resource Amount(Player, Amount, Count, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n| Count | a number of units, 1 to 255, or All (stored as 0) | byte 27 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nModify Unit Resource Amount(\"Player 12\", 1500, All, \"Main\");\n```\n\n### Modify Unit Shield Points\n\nSets the shields of the number of units of the type in the location to the percentage of\ntheir maximum.\n\n<!-- generated: action 51 -->\n**Action 51** · `Modify Unit Shield Points(Player, Unit, Percent, Count, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Percent | a percentage, 0 to 100 | bytes 20–23 |\n| Count | a number of units, 1 to 255, or All (stored as 0) | byte 27 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nModify Unit Shield Points(\"Current Player\", \"Protoss Zealot\", 100, All, \"Anywhere\");\n```\n\n### Move Location\n\nCentres the second location (*Move*) on a unit of the type owned by the player inside the\nfirst (*Unit at*). Following a unit with a location, so that other triggers can act around\nit, is done with this. With no such unit, the second location is centred on the first. The\nmoved location keeps its size and is kept inside the map. Anywhere can be moved too.\n\n<!-- generated: action 38 -->\n**Action 38** · `Move Location(Player, Unit, Unit at, Move);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Unit at | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n| Move | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 20–23 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nMove Location(\"Current Player\", \"Terran Marine\", \"Anywhere\", \"Hero\");\n```\n\n### Move Unit\n\nMoves the number of units of the type from one location to the middle of another, at once.\nWith a group as the player, the count is for the whole group. A unit that does not fit\nthere goes to the nearest spot it fits.\n\n<!-- generated: action 39 -->\n**Action 39** · `Move Unit(Player, Unit, Count, From, To);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Count | a number of units, 1 to 255, or All (stored as 0) | byte 27 |\n| From | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n| To | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 20–23 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nMove Unit(\"Current Player\", \"Men\", All, \"Teleporter\", \"Exit\");\n```\n\n### Mute Unit Speech\n\nSilences the voices of units, so that a transmission or a sound can be heard over them.\n[Unmute Unit Speech](#unmute-unit-speech) turns them back on.\n\n<!-- generated: action 30 -->\n**Action 30** · `Mute Unit Speech();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nMute Unit Speech();\n```\n\n### Order\n\nOrders the player's units of the type in one location to move, patrol or attack towards\nthe middle of another. It orders all of them; there is no count.\n\n<!-- generated: action 46 -->\n**Action 46** · `Order(Player, Unit, From, To, Order);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| From | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n| To | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 20–23 |\n| Order | [move, patrol or attack](#orders) | byte 27 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nOrder(\"Player 8\", \"Men\", \"Spawn\", \"Base\", attack);\n```\n\n### Pause Game\n\nPauses the game. It works in single player only, and only for a person's trigger; in a\nmultiplayer game it does nothing. The triggers keep running while the game is paused:\nwaits count down and passes go on, but the game clock and the countdown stop. The game is\nunpaused when the trigger that paused it finishes.\n\n<!-- generated: action 5 -->\n**Action 5** · `Pause Game();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nPause Game();\n```\n\n### Pause Timer\n\nStops the countdown timer where it is.\n\n<!-- generated: action 54 -->\n**Action 54** · `Pause Timer();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nPause Timer();\n```\n\n### Play WAV\n\nPlays a sound for the player. The file is one stored in the map archive, which the\neditor's Sound Editor adds, or one of the game's own sounds by its path, such as\n`sound\\Terran\\Marine\\TMaYes00.wav`. A sound of the game's needs no file in the map; the\nTrigger Editor's **Browse…** lists them. Duration is the sound's length in milliseconds,\nwhich editors fill in from the file. The game does not use it: Play WAV never holds the\ntrigger, so the next action runs while the sound plays. Only the player the trigger runs\nfor hears it.\n\n<!-- generated: action 8 -->\n**Action 8** · `Play WAV(WAV, Duration);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| WAV | the name of a sound file in the map, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 8–11 |\n| Duration | a time in milliseconds | bytes 12–15 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nPlay WAV(\"staredit\\\\wav\\\\alarm.wav\", 1500);\n```\n\n### Preserve Trigger\n\nKeeps the trigger after it runs, so it runs again on every cycle its conditions hold.\nWithout it a trigger runs once for each player it runs for. The trigger's Preserved flag\n(0x04) does the same.\n\n<!-- generated: action 3 -->\n**Action 3** · `Preserve Trigger();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nPreserve Trigger();\n```\n\n### Remove Unit\n\nRemoves every unit of the type the player owns, anywhere on the map, without a death\nanimation. Like [Kill Unit](#kill-unit), it adds nothing to death counts, kills or score.\n\n<!-- generated: action 24 -->\n**Action 24** · `Remove Unit(Player, Unit);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nRemove Unit(\"Player 12\", \"Map Revealer\");\n```\n\n### Remove Unit At Location\n\nAs [Remove Unit](#remove-unit), for the number of units of the type in the location.\n\n<!-- generated: action 25 -->\n**Action 25** · `Remove Unit At Location(Player, Unit, Count, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Count | a number of units, 1 to 255, or All (stored as 0) | byte 27 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nRemove Unit At Location(\"All Players\", \"Men\", All, \"Void\");\n```\n\n### Run AI Script\n\nStarts an [AI script](#ai-scripts) for the player, which is normally a computer player:\nthe custom-level scripts that make it play a normal game, or one of the small scripts that\nchange its behaviour. For anyone but a computer player (a person, a rescuable slot or a\nneutral slot) the game runs only the scripts that make sense for them: the bunker,\ntransport and web scripts (EnBk, EnTr, ExTr, DWHe, PB3F) and the shared-vision ones (+Vi\nand −Vi), and for a neutral slot also Rscu. It ignores any other.\n\n<!-- generated: action 15 -->\n**Action 15** · `Run AI Script(Script);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Script | an [AI script](#ai-scripts), stored as its four-letter code | bytes 20–23 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nRun AI Script(\"Terran Expansion Custom Level\");\n```\n\n### Run AI Script At Location\n\nAs [Run AI Script](#run-ai-script), for the scripts that act on a place, such as *Value\nThis Area Higher* or *Make These Units Patrol*.\n\n<!-- generated: action 16 -->\n**Action 16** · `Run AI Script At Location(Script, Location);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Script | an [AI script](#ai-scripts), stored as its four-letter code | bytes 20–23 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nRun AI Script At Location(\"Value This Area Higher\", \"Expansion\");\n```\n\n### Set Alliance Status\n\nSets how the player the trigger runs for stands towards the Player argument: enemy, ally,\nor ally for victory. Any status value other than ally and allied victory makes them\nenemies.\n\nMaking a player an ally, or an ally for victory, also changes every other player the\ntrigger's player was already allied with to the same kind of alliance.\n\n<!-- generated: action 57 -->\n**Action 57** · `Set Alliance Status(Player, Status);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Status | [Enemy, Ally or Allied Victory](#alliances) | bytes 24–25 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nSet Alliance Status(\"Force 1\", Allied Victory);\n```\n\n### Set Countdown Timer\n\nSets the countdown timer shown at the top of the screen, adds to it or takes from it, in\nseconds. [Countdown Timer](#countdown-timer) tests it. The timer stays between 0 and\n359,999 seconds. When it reaches 0 the game goes through the triggers on the next frame.\n\n<!-- generated: action 14 -->\n**Action 14** · `Set Countdown Timer(Modifier, Seconds);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Modifier | [Set To, Add or Subtract](#modifiers) | byte 27 |\n| Seconds | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 12–15 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nSet Countdown Timer(Set To, 300);\n```\n\n### Set Deaths\n\nChanges the player's [death count](#deaths) for the unit type. It is how maps keep\nnumbers: nothing but Set Deaths and units dying in the game changes a death count, so the\ncounts of unit types a map never uses can hold anything.\n\nAdd past 4,294,967,295 wraps around to 0, and Subtract stops at 0. It writes nothing for a\nplayer above Player 8 or a unit number above 232, so in 1.16.1 it cannot reach memory\noutside the table (see [EUD](#eud)). Remastered is the same unless the map uses EUD\ntriggers.\n\nThe unit classes do not work as counters. Set Deaths of Any unit writes nothing. Men,\nBuildings and Factories change the player's real counts of those lost, the ones the game\nadds to whenever such a unit dies, so a number kept there is mixed with real deaths (see\n[Deaths and kills](#deaths-and-kills)).\n\n<!-- generated: action 45 -->\n**Action 45** · `Set Deaths(Player, Unit, Modifier, Amount);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Modifier | [Set To, Add or Subtract](#modifiers) | byte 27 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nSet Deaths(\"Current Player\", \"Cave (Unused)\", Add, 1);\n```\n\n### Set Doodad State\n\nTurns doodad units in the location on or off: the doors and traps of the Installation and\nother tilesets, which the map places as units. It changes only the first such unit it\nfinds in the location, not all of them.\n\n<!-- generated: action 42 -->\n**Action 42** · `Set Doodad State(Player, Unit, Location, State);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n| State | [enable, disable or toggle](#states) | byte 27 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nSet Doodad State(\"All Players\", \"Right Upper Level Door\", \"Door\", disable);\n```\n\n### Set Invincibility\n\nMakes the player's units of the type in the location invincible, or takes it away. Units\naboard the player's transports there are changed too. A unit type that is invincible in\nthe game's own data stays invincible.\n\n<!-- generated: action 43 -->\n**Action 43** · `Set Invincibility(Player, Unit, Location, State);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n| State | [enable, disable or toggle](#states) | byte 27 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nSet Invincibility(\"Player 8\", \"Buildings\", \"Anywhere\", enable);\n```\n\n### Set Mission Objectives\n\nSets the text of the player's mission objectives screen.\n\n<!-- generated: action 12 -->\n**Action 12** · `Set Mission Objectives(Text);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Text | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nSet Mission Objectives(\"Destroy the Zerg hive.\");\n```\n\n### Set Next Scenario\n\nChooses the map that follows when the player wins, as a campaign does. The text is the\nscenario's name.\n\n<!-- generated: action 41 -->\n**Action 41** · `Set Next Scenario(Scenario);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Scenario | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nSet Next Scenario(\"Episode 2\");\n```\n\n### Set Resources\n\nSets the player's ore, gas, or both, adds to it or takes from it. With *ore and gas*, each\ngets the whole amount. Add past 4,294,967,295 wraps around to 0, and Subtract stops at 0.\nBlizzard's melee maps give every player their starting minerals with it, when they are\nplayed as Use Map Settings.\n\n<!-- generated: action 26 -->\n**Action 26** · `Set Resources(Player, Modifier, Amount, Resource);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Modifier | [Set To, Add or Subtract](#modifiers) | byte 27 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n| Resource | [ore, gas, or ore and gas](#resources) | bytes 24–25 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nSet Resources(\"Current Player\", Set To, 50, ore);\n```\n\n### Set Score\n\nSets the player's score of the [kind](#scores), adds to it or takes from it. Custom is the\nscore maps keep for themselves, often shown with [Leader Board\nPoints](#leader-board-points). A kind that is a sum of others is shared out between them:\nTotal adds or takes a quarter of the amount, rounded up, to each of units, buildings,\nkills and razings (Custom is left alone), and the two kinds that are pairs half to each.\n\n<!-- generated: action 27 -->\n**Action 27** · `Set Score(Player, Modifier, Amount, Score);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Player | a [player or group](#players-and-groups) | bytes 16–19 |\n| Modifier | [Set To, Add or Subtract](#modifiers) | byte 27 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n| Score | a [score type](#scores) | bytes 24–25 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nSet Score(\"Current Player\", Add, 10, Custom);\n```\n\n### Set Switch\n\nSets, clears, toggles or randomizes the [switch](#switches). Randomize sets or clears it\nat random, with one draw of the game's random numbers, the same on every machine.\n\n<!-- generated: action 13 -->\n**Action 13** · `Set Switch(Switch, Action);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Switch | one of the 256 [switches](#switches) | bytes 20–23 |\n| Action | [set, clear, toggle or randomize](#switches) | byte 27 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nSet Switch(\"Switch 1\", set);\n```\n\n### Talking Portrait\n\nShows the unit type's portrait talking in the player's portrait box for the time. It does\nnot hold the trigger. Only the player the trigger runs for sees it.\n\n<!-- generated: action 29 -->\n**Action 29** · `Talking Portrait(Unit, Duration);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Duration | a time in milliseconds | bytes 12–15 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nTalking Portrait(\"Jim Raynor (Marine)\", 3000);\n```\n\n### Transmission\n\nA message from a unit: the unit type's portrait talks, the text is shown and the sound\nplays. The first unit of the type in the location, whoever owns it, blinks and is pinged\non the minimap; with none there, nothing is pinged. Like a [Wait](#wait), it holds the\nrest of the trigger until it is over, in the player's wait slot. How long that is starts\nfrom the sound's length (*WAV duration*) and is changed by the modifier and *Duration*:\nSet To replaces it, Add and Subtract change it.\n\nIn a single-player game it holds until the portrait has finished talking; in a multiplayer\ngame for that time, in milliseconds of game speed. For a computer player it shows nothing\nand holds nothing, and without a location it does nothing at all.\n\n<!-- generated: action 7 -->\n**Action 7** · `Transmission(Display, Text, Unit, Location, Modifier, Duration, WAV, WAV duration);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Display | Always Display or Don't Always Display | bit 0x04 of byte 28 |\n| Text | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Location | a location (Anywhere is location 64) | bytes 0–3 |\n| Modifier | [Set To, Add or Subtract](#modifiers) | byte 27 |\n| Duration | a time in milliseconds | bytes 20–23 |\n| WAV | the name of a sound file in the map, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 8–11 |\n| WAV duration | a time in milliseconds | bytes 12–15 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nTransmission(Always Display, \"Hold this position.\", \"Jim Raynor (Marine)\", \"Base\", Add, 0, \"staredit\\\\wav\\\\raynor.wav\", 2400);\n```\n\n### Unmute Unit Speech\n\nLets unit voices play again after [Mute Unit Speech](#mute-unit-speech).\n\n<!-- generated: action 31 -->\n**Action 31** · `Unmute Unit Speech();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nUnmute Unit Speech();\n```\n\n### Unpause Game\n\nResumes a game that [Pause Game](#pause-game) stopped. Single player only.\n\n<!-- generated: action 6 -->\n**Action 6** · `Unpause Game();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nUnpause Game();\n```\n\n### Unpause Timer\n\nRestarts the countdown timer after [Pause Timer](#pause-timer).\n\n<!-- generated: action 55 -->\n**Action 55** · `Unpause Timer();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nUnpause Timer();\n```\n\n### Victory\n\nThe player wins. Every other player still in the game who has no result in the same pass\nis defeated, and if every player wins in the same pass it is a draw for all. See [Winning\nand losing](#winning-and-losing).\n\n<!-- generated: action 1 -->\n**Action 1** · `Victory();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nVictory();\n```\n\n### Wait\n\nHolds the rest of the trigger's actions for the time, in milliseconds at the game's speed:\nWait(1000) is 25 frames at Fastest and 16 at Normal. See [Waits](#waits) for the frames at\nevery speed and how a player's waits hold each other up, and [Hyper\ntriggers](#hyper-triggers) for Wait(0).\n\n<!-- generated: action 4 -->\n**Action 4** · `Wait(Milliseconds);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Milliseconds | a time in milliseconds | bytes 12–15 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n```trigedit\nWait(1000);\n```\n\n### Disable Debug Mode\n\nIn the game's list of actions, but StarEdit does not offer it. It stops the Esc key from\nskipping this trigger: in a single-player game Esc normally skips the person's running\ntriggers through their Waits, transmissions, text and sounds, as for a cinematic. [Enable\nDebug Mode](#enable-debug-mode) allows it again.\n\n<!-- generated: action 58 -->\n**Action 58** · `Disable Debug Mode();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Enable Debug Mode\n\nLets the Esc key skip this trigger again after [Disable Debug Mode](#disable-debug-mode).\nNot offered by StarEdit.\n\n<!-- generated: action 59 -->\n**Action 59** · `Enable Debug Mode();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n## Mission briefing actions\n\nA mission briefing is the screen before a single-player mission: portraits, text and\nspeech, one briefing per player. It is stored in the map's `MBRF` section, in the same\nrecord as a trigger, with the condition [Mission Briefing](#mission-briefing) and its own\nset of actions, numbered separately from a trigger's. Four portrait slots, 0 to 3, hold\nthe portraits. The editor's Triggers ▸ Mission Briefing Editor… edits them.\n\nThe game keeps the briefing triggers owned by the person playing, by All Players, or by\nthe person's force, and plays a briefing trigger only when its first condition is Mission\nBriefing. A briefing written as text without that condition never plays; Check Map points\nit out. (Read in 1.16.1's code; not checked in a game.)\n\n### Wait (briefing)\n\nHolds the briefing for the time.\n\n<!-- generated: briefing 1 -->\n**Briefing action 1** · `Wait(Milliseconds);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the briefing action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Milliseconds | a time in milliseconds | bytes 12–15 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Play WAV (briefing)\n\nPlays a sound.\n\n<!-- generated: briefing 2 -->\n**Briefing action 2** · `Play WAV(WAV, Duration);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the briefing action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| WAV | the name of a sound file in the map, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 8–11 |\n| Duration | a time in milliseconds | bytes 12–15 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Text Message\n\nShows the text for the time.\n\n<!-- generated: briefing 3 -->\n**Briefing action 3** · `Text Message(Text, Duration);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the briefing action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Text | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Duration | a time in milliseconds | bytes 12–15 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Mission Objectives\n\nSets the text of the objectives shown with the briefing.\n\n<!-- generated: briefing 4 -->\n**Briefing action 4** · `Mission Objectives(Text);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the briefing action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Text | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Show Portrait\n\nShows the unit type's portrait in a slot.\n\n<!-- generated: briefing 5 -->\n**Briefing action 5** · `Show Portrait(Unit, Slot);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the briefing action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | a unit type, or one of the [unit classes](#unit-classes) | bytes 24–25 |\n| Slot | a portrait slot, 0 to 3 | bytes 16–19 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Hide Portrait\n\nHides the portrait in a slot.\n\n<!-- generated: briefing 6 -->\n**Briefing action 6** · `Hide Portrait(Slot);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the briefing action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slot | a portrait slot, 0 to 3 | bytes 16–19 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Display Speaking Portrait\n\nMakes the portrait in a slot talk for the time.\n\n<!-- generated: briefing 7 -->\n**Briefing action 7** · `Display Speaking Portrait(Slot, Duration);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the briefing action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slot | a portrait slot, 0 to 3 | bytes 16–19 |\n| Duration | a time in milliseconds | bytes 12–15 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Transmission (briefing)\n\nText and a sound with the portrait in a slot talking, as a trigger's\n[Transmission](#transmission) does: the modifier and amount change the time, as they do\nthere. No Blizzard map uses it; its layout matches what 1.16.1's code reads.\n\n<!-- generated: briefing 8 -->\n**Briefing action 8** · `Transmission(Text, Slot, Modifier, Amount, Duration, WAV);`\n\n| Argument | Takes | Stored in the briefing action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Text | text, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 4–7 |\n| Slot | a portrait slot, 0 to 3 | bytes 16–19 |\n| Modifier | [Set To, Add or Subtract](#modifiers) | byte 27 |\n| Amount | a whole number, 0 to 4,294,967,295 | bytes 20–23 |\n| Duration | a time in milliseconds | bytes 12–15 |\n| WAV | the name of a sound file in the map, stored as a string-table entry | bytes 8–11 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Skip Tutorial Enabled\n\nOffers the button that skips the tutorial. It works only in a single-player campaign.\n\n<!-- generated: briefing 9 -->\n**Briefing action 9** · `Skip Tutorial Enabled();`\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n## Players and groups\n\nThe sections from here on are reference tables. These are the values a trigger stores for\na player. The same 27 values number the owners a trigger can have (one byte each in the\nrecord) and are what the Player arguments of conditions and actions store.\n\n<!-- generated: players -->\n| Player or group | Stored as |\n| --- | --- |\n| Player 1 (also `P1`) | 0 |\n| Player 2 (also `P2`) | 1 |\n| Player 3 (also `P3`) | 2 |\n| Player 4 (also `P4`) | 3 |\n| Player 5 (also `P5`) | 4 |\n| Player 6 (also `P6`) | 5 |\n| Player 7 (also `P7`) | 6 |\n| Player 8 (also `P8`) | 7 |\n| Player 9 (also `P9`) | 8 |\n| Player 10 (also `P10`) | 9 |\n| Player 11 (also `P11`) | 10 |\n| Player 12 (also `P12`) | 11 |\n| None (also `Player 13`) | 12 |\n| Current Player | 13 |\n| Foes | 14 |\n| Allies | 15 |\n| Neutral Players | 16 |\n| All Players (also `All players`) | 17 |\n| Force 1 | 18 |\n| Force 2 | 19 |\n| Force 3 | 20 |\n| Force 4 | 21 |\n| Unused 1 | 22 |\n| Unused 2 | 23 |\n| Unused 3 | 24 |\n| Unused 4 | 25 |\n| Non Allied Victory Players (also `Non AV Players`) | 26 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\nPlayers 1 to 8 are the seats a person or a computer plays in. Players 9 to 12 are not;\nPlayer 12 is the neutral player, which owns the resources and critters a map places as\nNeutral. **Current Player** is the player the trigger is running for (see [Who a trigger\nruns for](#who-a-trigger-runs-for)). **Foes** and **Allies** are counted from that\nplayer's point of view: **Allies** are the players allied *for victory* with it, and\nplayers who are plain allies count as **Neutral Players**. A rescuable or neutral slot is\nin the game like anyone else, so it is counted in All Players, in its force and in every\nMost and Least comparison. The four **Unused** groups have no players behind them.\n\n**All Players** as a condition's or action's player means more than the players in the\ngame. In a condition it also takes in Players 9 to 12, always, so Command(All Players, …)\ncounts Player 12's units too. In actions that act on units, such as Kill Unit, Give Units\nto Player or Modify Unit Hit Points, it reaches Players 9 to 12's units as well. Create\nUnit, Set Deaths, Set Resources and Set Score do nothing for Players 9 to 12, whether they\nare named or reached through All Players, except Set Deaths in Remastered's [EUD\nmode](#eud). As a trigger's owner, All Players is only the players in the game.\n\n**None** is no player. As a condition's player it reads somewhere unexpected, and a player\nvalue past this table reads outside the game's tables; see [EUD](#eud).\n\n## Argument values\n\nThese are the values the enumerated arguments take, and the number each is stored as. The\nnames in parentheses are the other spellings the editor's text format accepts.\n\n### Comparisons\n\n<!-- generated: values comparison -->\n| Name | Stored as |\n| --- | --- |\n| At least (also `atleast`, `>=`) | 0 |\n| At most (also `atmost`, `<=`) | 1 |\n| Exactly (also `==`) | 10 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Modifiers\n\nSet To replaces the value, Add and Subtract change it.\n\n<!-- generated: values modifier -->\n| Name | Stored as |\n| --- | --- |\n| Set To (also `setto`, `set`) | 7 |\n| Add | 8 |\n| Subtract (also `sub`) | 9 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Switches\n\nA map has 256 switches, each either set or clear, and every one starts clear. The\n[Switch](#switch) condition tests one and [Set Switch](#set-switch) changes it. Their\nnames are for the editor; the game only uses the numbers.\n\nThe Switch condition's states:\n\n<!-- generated: values switchState -->\n| Name | Stored as |\n| --- | --- |\n| set (also `true`) | 2 |\n| not set (also `cleared`, `clear`, `false`) | 3 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\nSet Switch's actions:\n\n<!-- generated: values switchAction -->\n| Name | Stored as |\n| --- | --- |\n| set | 4 |\n| clear (also `cleared`) | 5 |\n| toggle | 6 |\n| randomize (also `random`, `randomise`) | 11 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Resources\n\n<!-- generated: values resource -->\n| Name | Stored as |\n| --- | --- |\n| ore (also `minerals`) | 0 |\n| gas | 1 |\n| ore and gas (also `both`) | 2 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Scores\n\nCustom is a score only triggers change, with [Set Score](#set-score).\n\n<!-- generated: values score -->\n| Name | Stored as |\n| --- | --- |\n| Total | 0 |\n| Units | 1 |\n| Buildings | 2 |\n| Units and buildings | 3 |\n| Kills | 4 |\n| Razings | 5 |\n| Kills and razings | 6 |\n| Custom | 7 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Orders\n\n<!-- generated: values order -->\n| Name | Stored as |\n| --- | --- |\n| move | 0 |\n| patrol | 1 |\n| attack | 2 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Alliances\n\n<!-- generated: values alliance -->\n| Name | Stored as |\n| --- | --- |\n| Enemy | 0 |\n| Ally (also `Allied`) | 1 |\n| Allied Victory | 2 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### States\n\nFor Set Doodad State, Set Invincibility and Leaderboard Computer Players.\n\n<!-- generated: values unitState -->\n| Name | Stored as |\n| --- | --- |\n| enable (also `enabled`) | 4 |\n| disable (also `disabled`) | 5 |\n| toggle | 6 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### Unit classes\n\nUnit arguments take a unit type (0 to 227) or one of these classes, which stand for a\nwhole group of types. A melee map's defeat trigger is \"commands at most 0 Buildings\".\n\n<!-- generated: values unitClass -->\n| Class | Stored as |\n| --- | --- |\n| Any unit (also `Any Unit`) | 229 |\n| Men | 230 |\n| Buildings | 231 |\n| Factories | 232 |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\nIn the text form a unit is written by its name: StarEdit's name for the game's own data,\nor the data set's name when one is in use, so a mod's \"Terran Civilian\" reads as the mod's\ntype and not the game's type of that name. A name two types share, or a class's name, is\nwritten as `\"Unit #332\"` instead, which always reads back as that number.\n\n### AI scripts\n\nThe scripts StarEdit offers. An action stores the script's four-letter code; the\ncampaign's own scripts are there too, under codes this table does not name.\n\n<!-- generated: values aiScript -->\n| Code | Script |\n| --- | --- |\n| `TMCu` | Terran Custom Level |\n| `ZMCu` | Zerg Custom Level |\n| `PMCu` | Protoss Custom Level |\n| `TMCx` | Terran Expansion Custom Level |\n| `ZMCx` | Zerg Expansion Custom Level |\n| `PMCx` | Protoss Expansion Custom Level |\n| `TLOf` | Terran Campaign Easy |\n| `TMED` | Terran Campaign Medium |\n| `THIf` | Terran Campaign Difficult |\n| `TSUP` | Terran Campaign Insane |\n| `TARE` | Terran Campaign Area Town |\n| `ZLOf` | Zerg Campaign Easy |\n| `ZMED` | Zerg Campaign Medium |\n| `ZHIf` | Zerg Campaign Difficult |\n| `ZSUP` | Zerg Campaign Insane |\n| `ZARE` | Zerg Campaign Area Town |\n| `PLOf` | Protoss Campaign Easy |\n| `PMED` | Protoss Campaign Medium |\n| `PHIf` | Protoss Campaign Difficult |\n| `PSUP` | Protoss Campaign Insane |\n| `PARE` | Protoss Campaign Area Town |\n| `TLOx` | Expansion Terran Campaign Easy |\n| `TMEx` | Expansion Terran Campaign Medium |\n| `THIx` | Expansion Terran Campaign Difficult |\n| `TSUx` | Expansion Terran Campaign Insane |\n| `TARx` | Expansion Terran Campaign Area Town |\n| `ZLOx` | Expansion Zerg Campaign Easy |\n| `ZMEx` | Expansion Zerg Campaign Medium |\n| `ZHIx` | Expansion Zerg Campaign Difficult |\n| `ZSUx` | Expansion Zerg Campaign Insane |\n| `ZARx` | Expansion Zerg Campaign Area Town |\n| `PLOx` | Expansion Protoss Campaign Easy |\n| `PMEx` | Expansion Protoss Campaign Medium |\n| `PHIx` | Expansion Protoss Campaign Difficult |\n| `PSUx` | Expansion Protoss Campaign Insane |\n| `PARx` | Expansion Protoss Campaign Area Town |\n| `Suic` | Send All Units on Strategic Suicide Missions |\n| `SuiR` | Send All Units on Random Suicide Missions |\n| `Rscu` | Switch Computer Player to Rescue Passive |\n| `+Vi0` | Turn ON Shared Vision for Player 1 |\n| `+Vi1` | Turn ON Shared Vision for Player 2 |\n| `+Vi2` | Turn ON Shared Vision for Player 3 |\n| `+Vi3` | Turn ON Shared Vision for Player 4 |\n| `+Vi4` | Turn ON Shared Vision for Player 5 |\n| `+Vi5` | Turn ON Shared Vision for Player 6 |\n| `+Vi6` | Turn ON Shared Vision for Player 7 |\n| `+Vi7` | Turn ON Shared Vision for Player 8 |\n| `-Vi0` | Turn OFF Shared Vision for Player 1 |\n| `-Vi1` | Turn OFF Shared Vision for Player 2 |\n| `-Vi2` | Turn OFF Shared Vision for Player 3 |\n| `-Vi3` | Turn OFF Shared Vision for Player 4 |\n| `-Vi4` | Turn OFF Shared Vision for Player 5 |\n| `-Vi5` | Turn OFF Shared Vision for Player 6 |\n| `-Vi6` | Turn OFF Shared Vision for Player 7 |\n| `-Vi7` | Turn OFF Shared Vision for Player 8 |\n| `MvTe` | Move Dark Templars to Region |\n| `ClrC` | Clear Previous Combat Data |\n| `Enmy` | Set Player to Enemy |\n| `Ally` | Set Player to Ally |\n| `VluA` | Value This Area Higher |\n| `EnBk` | Enter Closest Bunker |\n| `StTg` | Set Generic Command Target |\n| `StPt` | Make These Units Patrol |\n| `EnTr` | Enter Transport |\n| `ExTr` | Exit Transport |\n| `NuHe` | AI Nuke Here |\n| `HaHe` | AI Harass Here |\n| `JYDg` | Set Unit Order To: Junk Yard Dog |\n| `DWHe` | Disruption Web Here |\n| `ReHe` | Recall Here |\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n## The trigger record\n\nTriggers are stored in the map's `TRIG` section, one 2400-byte record per trigger, in the\norder of the list. Mission briefings are in `MBRF`, in the same record with a different\nset of actions.\n\n### A trigger\n\n![The trigger record, byte by byte](images/chk-trigger-record.svg)\n\n| Bytes | Holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| 0–319 | 16 conditions, 20 bytes each |\n| 320–2367 | 64 actions, 32 bytes each |\n| 2368–2371 | the trigger's flags |\n| 2372–2398 | 27 bytes, one per [player or group](#players-and-groups): non-zero where the trigger runs for it |\n| 2399 | used by the game while the trigger runs; 0 in a saved map |\n\nThe trigger's flags:\n\n| Bit | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| 0x04 | Preserved: the same as a [Preserve Trigger](#preserve-trigger) action |\n| 0x08 | Disabled, or finished: the trigger never runs. Editors set it to disable a trigger, and the game sets it on a trigger that has run and is not preserved |\n| 0x02 | Each time the trigger's actions run, the player counts as having won without winning: every other player still in the game with no result is defeated, and the player's game goes on. A Defeat or Draw in the same trigger never takes effect. Some editors call it \"ignore Defeat and Draw\", which is not what it does |\n| 0x01 | The trigger is part way through its actions; byte 2399 says which is next |\n| 0x10 | Esc is skipping the trigger's Waits, transmissions and text |\n| 0x20 | The trigger paused the game, and unpauses it when it finishes |\n| 0x40 | Esc may not skip the trigger ([Disable Debug Mode](#disable-debug-mode)) |\n\nThe game sets 0x01, 0x10, 0x20 and 0x40 itself while the trigger runs, and StarEdit saves\nthem as 0. A file that sets them is still obeyed: with 0x01 set, the first pass skips the\nconditions and starts at the action byte 2399 names.\n\nThe list of conditions ends at the first slot whose type is 0, and so does the list of\nactions.\n\n### A condition\n\n<!-- generated: layout condition -->\n| Bytes | Holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| 0–3 | Location number, counted from 1; 0 for none. |\n| 4–7 | Player or group. |\n| 8–11 | Amount compared against. |\n| 12–13 | Unit type. |\n| 14 | Comparison, or the switch state for Switch. |\n| 15 | Which condition this is. |\n| 16 | Resource type, score type or switch number, depending on the condition. |\n| 17 | Flags: 0x02 disabled; the others are editor and game bookkeeping. |\n| 18–19 | Unused by ordinary triggers; some EUD tools store a mask here. |\n\nAll numbers are little-endian. An unused condition slot is all zeros.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n### An action\n\n<!-- generated: layout action -->\n| Bytes | Holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| 0–3 | Location number, counted from 1; 0 for none. The source location where there are two. |\n| 4–7 | String-table entry of the text. |\n| 8–11 | String-table entry of the sound file's name. |\n| 12–15 | A time: milliseconds, or seconds for Set Countdown Timer. |\n| 16–19 | Player or group; the first player where there are two. |\n| 20–23 | Second player, destination location, amount, properties slot or AI script, depending on the action. |\n| 24–25 | Unit type, or the score, resource or alliance type. |\n| 26 | Which action this is. |\n| 27 | Unit count, modifier, switch action, order or state, depending on the action. |\n| 28 | Flags: 0x02 disabled, 0x04 always display; the others are editor and game bookkeeping. |\n| 29 | Unused. |\n| 30–31 | Unused by ordinary triggers; some EUD tools store a mask here. |\n\nAll numbers are little-endian. An unused action slot is all zeros.\n<!-- /generated -->\n\n## EUD\n\nAn *EUD* trigger uses a player or unit value outside the game's tables to reach other\nparts of the game's memory. The editor's player pick has an EPD box that turns a memory\naddress into the player value that reaches it.\n\nA condition with a player value past the [players and groups](#players-and-groups) table\nreads memory outside its table. Deaths reads the 4 bytes at 0x58A364 + 4 × player + 48 ×\nunit, for any player and unit number.\n\nIn 1.16.1 this works for reading only. [Set Deaths](#set-deaths), [Set\nResources](#set-resources) and [Set Score](#set-score) write nothing for a player above\nPlayer 8, and Set Deaths writes nothing for a unit number above 232, so 1.16.1 has no EUD\nwrites.\n\nStarCraft: Remastered has an EUD mode of its own. When it loads a map it looks through the\ntriggers, disabled ones included, for a Deaths condition or a Set Deaths action whose\nplayer value is above 26 or is 12 (None), or whose unit number is above 232. One is enough\nto turn the mode on for the whole map, and a Deaths condition with None as its player\ncounts. (Read in Remastered's code.)\n\nIn EUD mode, EUD conditions and Set Deaths use the addresses they would in 1.16.1, for the\nparts of the game's memory Remastered allows, and Set Deaths also writes Players 9 to 12's\ndeath counts. This is what EUD maps for Remastered, and the editor's TrigScript and\nMagenta plugins, are built on. In a map with no EUD trigger, Set Deaths writes nothing for\nPlayers 9 to 12, as in 1.16.1. (Found playing Remastered.)\n\n**None** as a condition's player does not check anything: it reads the cell after Player\n12's, which is Player 1's count of the *next* unit type. Deaths(None, unit 7) is Player\n1's deaths of unit 8, and Accumulate(None, ore) is Player 1's gas.\n\n## In the source\n\n| What | Where |\n| --- | --- |\n| The trigger record, its flags and the numbers of every condition and action | `src/formats/chk/sections/triggers.ts` |\n| Which field holds which argument, the argument values, the AI scripts | `src/data/triggerDefs.ts` |\n| The text form, printed and parsed | `src/formats/triggers/text.ts` |\n| The generated blocks of this page | `scripts/lib/trigger-reference.mjs`, written by `npm run docs:reference` |\n";
export const GUIDE_MD: string = "# User guide\n\nThis guide is for map makers. It starts with getting the editor running, walks through a\nfirst map, and then goes through each layer and dialog in turn. TrigScript, triggers\nwritten as code, has [a guide of its own](trigscript.md); the technical side (file\nformats, game data, writing plugins, building from source) is covered in separate\ndocuments, listed in the [README](../README.md#documentation). Several tasks here also\nhave a terminal form, in the [command line guide](cli.md); **Help ▸ Command Line…** opens\nits install page.\n\n> **Beta.** This editor needs to be extensively tested. Keep backups of maps you care about,\n> and check anything important in-game before you rely on it.\n\nYou do not need to read all of it. Pick the path that matches what you came to do:\n\n- **A first melee map.** [Getting started](#getting-started) to get the editor running,\n  then [Your first map](#your-first-map), which takes a blank map to a playable game in\n  six steps.\n- **A first scripted scenario.** [Your first map](#your-first-map) for the ground and the\n  bases, then [Triggers](#triggers) to write the rules in the Trigger Editor, or\n  TrigScript's [Quick start](trigscript.md#quick-start) to write them as code. Check them\n  with [Check Map](#check-map) and play them with [Test Map](#test-map).\n- **An existing project from SCMDraft 2.** [Coming from SCMDraft\n  2](#coming-from-scmdraft-2) says what carries over and what works differently.\n- **A map for a mod.** Add the mod as a data set ([Data sets made from a\n  mod](game-data.md#data-sets-made-from-a-mod)), and the palette, the settings and the\n  triggers follow it. The [mods guide](mods.md) covers the rest: the mod as a folder of\n  text, playing it on the play page with a bot, and its replays.\n\n## Getting started\n\nThere are three ways to run scmJS, each covered in [docs/installing.md](installing.md):\n\n- **In the browser** at [scmjs.dev/editor](https://scmjs.dev/editor/). Nothing to install,\n  and your maps stay on your own disk.\n- **As a desktop app** for Windows, macOS and Linux, from the\n  [releases](https://github.com/scm-js/scm-js/releases) page. It finds a StarCraft\n  installation on its own and opens a map on a double-click.\n- **In a container**, for a server on your own network:\n  `docker run --rm -p 8080:80 ghcr.io/scm-js/scm-js:latest`, then open\n  `http://localhost:8080`.\n\n### The game's graphics\n\nStarCraft's graphics do not ship with the editor. On first use it asks where to get them,\nand the recommended answer is **your StarCraft folder**: the classic game (1.16) or\nStarCraft: Remastered. Choose **Choose the StarCraft folder…** and pick the folder the\ngame is installed in, usually `C:\\Program Files (x86)\\StarCraft`. The browser asks whether\nto upload the folder; nothing leaves your computer, the files are only read. The desktop\napp looks for an installation on its own, and has a **Search this computer** button as\nwell. Picking a Remastered folder also turns on [Remastered\ngraphics](#classic-or-remastered-graphics).\n\nWithout StarCraft installed, the dialog offers Blizzard's free StarCraft map editor\ndownload (83 MB), which carries the graphics. You can also pick `StarDat.mpq` and\n`BrooDat.mpq` from a classic installation by hand. What each source allows:\n\n| Where the files come from | Edit maps | Watch replays | Test or play on scmjs.dev |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Your StarCraft folder, classic or Remastered | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| `StarDat.mpq` and `BrooDat.mpq` from a 1.16 installation | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Blizzard's map editor download | Yes | Yes | No |\n\nThe files are read once and a copy is kept for next time. [The\ngraphics](installing.md#the-graphics) has the steps in full. Without any graphics the\neditor still runs, with terrain in flat colours and units as coloured markers.\n\n**Help ▸ Game Data…** opens the same dialog later. It shows where the current copy came\nfrom and can remove it. Under *Data sets* it installs a mod's files beside the game's and\nswitches between them, and offers **Add StarCraft: Remastered…** when you have a classic\ncopy and no Remastered one. A tick keeps the extra files the play page needs for real-time\nlighting (about 200 MB more). [Data sets](game-data.md#data-sets) explains how mods are\ninstalled.\n\n### Opening a map\n\n- **Ctrl+O** (File ▸ Open…) opens a `.scm`, `.scx` or `.chk` file, and so does dropping\n  one on the window.\n- **File ▸ Open Recent** lists what you have had open before. In Chrome, Edge and the\n  desktop app it reopens the file straight from disk.\n- **File ▸ Find on scmscx.com…** searches the community map archive and opens the map you\n  pick. A link like `https://scmjs.dev/editor/scmscx/35b32Dsq`, with the id from the map's\n  address on scmscx.com, opens the editor with that map picked.\n- **File ▸ Open from scmjs.dev…** lists the maps kept on your scmjs.dev account, with\n  every revision you saved (see [Your scmjs.dev account](#your-scmjsdev-account)).\n- **File ▸ Open Replay…** opens a replay over the map it was played on (see\n  [Replays](#replays)).\n\nA map opens in its own tab, beside the ones already open (see [The editor\nwindow](#the-editor-window)). The blank map the editor starts on is the exception: the\nfirst map you open takes its place.\n\nA map that is damaged, or that a map protector has altered, is checked and repaired as it\nopens. See [Damaged and protected maps](#damaged-and-protected-maps).\n\n## The editor window\n\nThe window has nine parts, numbered in the picture below.\n\n![The editor window with its parts numbered](images/editor.webp)\n\n1. **Menu bar.** Every command is here, with its shortcut beside it. The dot at the right\n   end glows while the map has unsaved changes.\n2. **Toolbar.** New / open / save, undo and redo, cut / copy / paste, the active layer and\n   brush size, the grid, locations and fog-of-war toggles, symmetry, find, zoom, and\n   buttons for Player Settings, the Trigger Editor and Test Map.\n3. **Layer rail.** The seven layers, top to bottom: Terrain, Doodads, Units, Sprites,\n   Locations, Fog of War, Cut / Copy / Paste. The keys `T D U S L F C` switch between\n   them.\n4. **Palette.** What the active layer places: terrain types and brushes, doodads, unit\n   types, sprites, the location list, the fog players, the clipboard options.\n5. **Map.** Drag to paint or place, click to select, double-click for properties.\n   Right-click opens a context menu, or stops whatever you are placing.\n6. **Minimap.** Click or drag on it to move the view.\n7. **Layers panel.** The eye hides a layer's objects from the view; the padlock locks a\n   layer so its tools stop changing the map. Plugin overlays (Walkability, say) are listed\n   under it.\n8. **Properties.** What is selected, or what is under the cursor: for a tile its id,\n   group, elevation, walkability and buildability; for a unit its owner, position and\n   vitals; for a location its bounds and elevation flags.\n9. **Status bar.** The tile and pixel under the cursor, the tile id, map size, tileset,\n   layer and zoom, and a line saying what the last action did or why it was refused. At\n   the right end, your scmjs.dev account (or *Sign in to scmjs.dev*), and the *AI* cell\n   when the [AI](#the-ai) is on; each is a click to open.\n\n### Several maps\n\nOpen a second map and a row of tabs appears under the toolbar, one per open map, with a\nglowing dot on any that has unsaved changes. Click a tab to switch, or use the Window menu\n(Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+Shift+Tab in the desktop app). The × on a tab, a middle click, or File\n▸ Close Map closes that map, asking about unsaved changes first. Each map keeps its own\nundo history, selection and view, so switching back lands where you left off.\n\nWith one map open the row is hidden. To work on one map at a time, untick *Open each map\nin its own tab* in Preferences ▸ General; Open and New then replace the open map.\n\n### Moving around\n\n- **Scroll** with the mouse wheel (Shift for sideways), the arrow keys (Shift for half a\n  screen), a middle-button drag or the scrollbars, or click on the minimap. A drag that\n  reaches the edge of the window scrolls the view with it, so a stroke, a marquee or a\n  unit being moved can carry on past the screen. It speeds up the further past the edge\n  you push.\n- **Zoom** with Ctrl++ and Ctrl+−, or the toolbar's magnifiers. Ctrl+0 is 100%, and\n  Ctrl+Shift+0 fits the whole map in the window. Preferences ▸ View can make the wheel\n  zoom instead of scroll.\n- **The grid** is Ctrl+G. View ▸ Grid Settings… sets its spacing, colour and style.\n- **The panels** can be hidden from View ▸ Panels, and their widths dragged.\n\n### Undo\n\nEvery layer has its own selection and its own palette. Undo is Ctrl+Z and redo Ctrl+Y. The\nhistory is two hundred steps deep and shared across all the layers, so a terrain stroke\nand the units it stranded come back together. In a dialog such as Player Settings, the\ntriggers or the strings, nothing changes until OK or Apply, and each OK or Apply that\nchanged something is one more undo step (\"Edit triggers\", \"Edit settings\"), in the same\nhistory as the strokes around it.\n\n## Classic or Remastered graphics\n\n![The same view drawn with the classic graphics on the left and Remastered's on the right](images/remastered-compare.webp)\n\nThe editor can draw a map with the classic graphics or with StarCraft: Remastered's.\n**View ▸ Remastered Graphics** switches between them at any time, for the terrain, the\nunits and the sprites together. The switch changes only how the map is drawn. It does not\ntouch the map, and the brushes behave the same with it on or off.\n\nRemastered's pictures have twice the detail, which shows most when you zoom in: at 200%\nthey are drawn pixel for pixel, where the classic ones are each pixel doubled.\n\nThe Remastered graphics come from a Remastered installation:\n\n- If you chose your Remastered folder when the editor first asked for the game's files,\n  they are installed and already on.\n- If you installed from a classic copy or Blizzard's download, open **Help ▸ Game Data…**\n  and choose **Add StarCraft: Remastered…** under *Data sets*, then pick the folder\n  Remastered is installed in.\n\nThe editor keeps a copy of what it needs (a few hundred MB), so this happens once.\n[Installing](installing.md#the-graphics) has the steps in full.\n\nRemastered's files also add several hundred doodads to seven of the eight tilesets, and\nwith them installed those doodads are in the Doodads palette, whichever look you draw\nwith. A map that uses one depends on Remastered: it shows blank tiles in an editor with a\n1.16 copy, and uses tiles the 1.16 game does not have. Maps that keep to the older doodads\nare unaffected. **Tools ▸ Check Map…** says when a map uses any of these tiles, how many,\nand where the first one is.\n\n![Big Game Hunters' shore drawn with the Remastered graphics](images/remastered-water.webp)\n\nWith **View ▸ Animate Water** on, water ripples and lava shimmers as they do in\nRemastered; Desert's tar stays still, as it does in the game. **Preferences ▸ View ▸\nGraphics** sets how strongly and how fast, and whether the editor starts with the\nRemastered graphics on.\n\nA few things differ from the game: units are drawn without its lighting, the minimap keeps\nthe classic colours, and an exported map image has still water.\n[game-data.md](game-data.md#remastered-graphics) explains how the graphics are drawn.\n\n## Your first map\n\nThis section walks through a small melee map from start to finish. Everything here is\ncovered in more depth in the sections that follow.\n\n### 1. Make the map\n\nCtrl+N opens New Scenario. Pick a tileset (each is shown as a sample of its ground), a\nsize, and how many players. The terrain list under the size is what the whole map is\nfilled with, and the preview shows the result at its real scale. *Place automatically*\n(off by default) lays down one start location per player in a ring; tick it for this map.\n\n![The New Scenario dialog](images/new-scenario.webp)\n\nGive the map a name and a description, which players see in the game lobby, and press\nCreate.\n\n### 2. Shape the ground\n\nStay on the Terrain layer and the **Isometric** tab. Pick *High Dirt* (Badlands; every\ntileset has a high ground of its own), set the brush to 3×3, and drag across the map. The\ncliffs around the high ground are laid for you, and where a stroke meets a terrain the new\none cannot touch, the terrain in between is put in. Painting *Water* works the same way\nand gives you a shore.\n\n![A plateau and a lake painted with the isometric brush](images/tutorial-terrain.webp)\n\nThe brush size keys are `[` and `]`. Alt+click picks the terrain under the cursor. Ctrl+Z\ntakes back the whole stroke.\n\nRamps are not terrain: they are on the **Doodads** layer. Type `ramp` in its search box to\nlist them, click one, and click on a cliff edge; the ghost is red until the footprint sits\non ground it fits.\n\n### 3. Place the bases\n\nSwitch to the **Units** layer (`U`). The start locations are already there. In the\npalette, *Special* holds *Start Location* and *Resources* holds the mineral fields and the\nVespene Geyser, or type a name in the search box. Click a type to arm it, then click on\nthe map to place; Esc or a right-click stops placing.\n\n![A start location with minerals and a geyser](images/tutorial-base.webp)\n\nA mineral field holds 1500 minerals and a geyser 5000 gas when placed; double-click one to\nchange the amount, or select a whole line and set them all at once. Eight fields and one\ngeyser is the usual main.\n\n*Snap to grid* keeps buildings and resources on the tile grid where the game expects them.\nThe two ticks above it are the game's own placement rules; a red ghost means the game\nwould drop the unit when it loaded the map, and the status bar says why.\n\nFor a symmetric map, turn on symmetry with `M` or the toolbar and pick the mode beside it:\nwhat you paint and place is mirrored, and each copy of a start location or base goes to\nthe next player. The Melee Wizard plugin (Plugins ▸ Browse Plugins…) lays out whole bases\nwith the mineral line at the distance the game mines fastest from.\n\n### 4. Check the players\n\nScenario ▸ Player Settings… is where each slot's controller, race and colour live. A melee\nmap needs *Human* and *User Selectable* on every slot that has a start location; that is\nwhat a new map is set to already. Force Settings… groups them into teams for the lobby.\n\n> **Note.** A Use Map Settings map is different: give each player a fixed race. The game\n> treats a player left on *User Selectable* as a melee player, hands them a base and\n> workers, and removes every unit you placed for them. It also removes the units of a human\n> slot nobody takes in the lobby, so put decorations such as beacons on Player 12, the\n> neutral one.\n\n### 5. Check the map\n\nTools ▸ Check Map… lists what the game would object to: a start location with no player,\nunits it would leave out, a section it would refuse, and so on. Double-click a line to go\nto it.\n\nView ▸ Walkability (Ctrl+Shift+W) draws the ground as a unit sees it: which areas connect,\nwhere the chokes are and how wide, and which pockets cannot be reached at all. It updates\nas you edit.\n\n### 6. Save and play\n\nCtrl+S saves. A new map opens the Save dialog the first time, where the name and the file\nformat are chosen; `.scx` is a Brood War map. From then on Ctrl+S writes the file in place\n(Chrome, Edge, the desktop app) or downloads it (Firefox, Safari); the notice at the\nbottom right says which.\n\nTo try the map, press Ctrl+F5 (Tools ▸ Test Map). The game cannot be handed a map from the\noutside, so the editor writes the map into a `scmJS` folder under the game's *Maps*\nfolder, where **Single Player ▸ Custom Game** lists it; the desktop app then starts the\ngame as well. In the editor at scmjs.dev, Ctrl+F5 plays the map in a browser tab instead,\nuntil you choose StarCraft under **Edit ▸ Preferences ▸ Testing**. See [Test\nMap](#test-map).\n\nThat is a playable melee map. Triggers, briefings, custom unit settings and everything a\nUse Map Settings map needs are in the sections below.\n\n## Coming from SCMDraft 2\n\nMost of what you know from SCMDraft 2 applies here. This section covers what carries over,\nwhat is in a different place, and what to check before you move a map you care about.\n\n### What carries over\n\n- **Your maps.** Open the `.scx`, `.scm` or `.chk` file as it is; there is nothing to\n  convert. On save the editor rewrites only the parts of the file that changed (unless you\n  choose *Smallest that plays* when saving): your edits, and any fixes the Repair plugin\n  made to a damaged map when it opened (see [Damaged and protected\n  maps](#damaged-and-protected-maps)). Everything else, including sections and archive\n  files it does not understand, is saved exactly as it was.\n- **Text triggers.** The Text Trigger Editor uses SCMDraft's TrigEdit syntax, so trigger\n  text can be pasted in either direction. It is a plugin that starts off: turn on TrigEdit\n  in Plugins ▸ Manage Plugins… (see [The Text Trigger Editor](#the-text-trigger-editor)).\n- **`.trg` files.** File ▸ Import and Export read and write SCMDraft's `.trg` format. A\n  `.trg` stores string numbers rather than text, so use it only between copies of the same\n  map; use text triggers between different maps (see [Importing, exporting and checking\n  triggers](#importing-exporting-and-checking-triggers)).\n- **Undo depth.** The history keeps 200 steps by default, as SCMDraft does. You can change\n  this in Preferences.\n\n### What is different\n\n- **Layers have single-key shortcuts.** `T D U S L F C` switch between Terrain, Doodads,\n  Units, Sprites, Locations, Fog of War and Cut / Copy / Paste, and `[` and `]` change the\n  brush size. Every command can be given other keys on Preferences ▸ Hotkeys (see\n  [Keyboard and preferences](#keyboard-and-preferences)).\n- **Dialogs are in the undo history.** Each OK or Apply in Player Settings, the Trigger\n  Editor and the other dialogs is one undo step, in the same history as your terrain\n  strokes. A resize and a tileset change are one step each too.\n- **Saving depends on where the editor runs.** The desktop app and Chrome or Edge save\n  over the file you opened; the desktop app copies the old file to a `.bak` first. Firefox\n  and Safari download every save instead (see [Saving](#saving)).\n- **Plugins are different.** SCMDraft's plugins are Windows programs and do not run here.\n  scmJS has its own plugins, several of them on by default (see [Plugins](#plugins)).\n- **Symmetry has limits.** It does not mirror the Blend brush or pasting, and the Tile\n  brush does not flip edge tiles (see [What it does not do](#what-it-does-not-do)).\n\n### Before you move a project\n\n- Keep a copy of the map as SCMDraft last saved it.\n- Open the map and run [Check Map](#check-map). It lists what the game would refuse or\n  silently drop, which is worth knowing before you change anything.\n- Make one small change, save, and play the map in the game. Then open the saved file in\n  SCMDraft and check that SCMDraft still opens it as you expect.\n\n## Terrain\n\nTerrain is painted with four brushes, each a tab in the palette:\n\n| Brush | Paints |\n| --- | --- |\n| Isometric | Terrain types, laying the cliffs and edges around them. The one to use for almost everything. |\n| Rect | Flat ground of one terrain type, with StarEdit's random variation. No cliffs, no edges. |\n| Tile | Any single tile by number: cliff pieces, doodad tiles, anything the tileset has. |\n| Blend | One tile next to a tile you picked, chosen from those whose facing edge continues it. |\n\n![Cliffs and a shore laid by the isometric brush, with a 3×3 brush ghost](images/terrain-isometric.webp)\n\nDrag to paint. `[` and `]` resize the brush from 1×1 to 7×7, Alt+click picks the terrain\nor tile under the cursor, and right-click offers Pick and Fill Area. The Properties panel\nbreaks the tile under the cursor into group, slot, elevation, walkability and\nbuildability.\n\n### The isometric brush\n\nThe isometric brush is StarEdit's diamond brush. It sets the diamond under the cursor to a\nterrain and works outward from there: a neighbour that cannot border the new terrain\nbecomes the terrain in between, and cliff faces stack as tall as the tileset draws them. A\nsingle click of high ground gives a small mesa; a wider brush gives flat high ground\ninside a cliff ring.\n\nThe brush works from the map's isometric record, a part of the file that only editors\nread. Protected maps often have it stripped; the Repair plugin rebuilds it as such a map\nopens, exactly where the ground was laid with the isometric brush and as a best guess\nelsewhere. The Isometric tab warns when the record and the ground disagree, and when part\nof the map is ground the brush cannot join up with, such as hand-placed tiles or another\neditor's work.\n\n### Rect, Tile and Blend\n\n![The Rect tab](images/palette-rect.webp) ![The Tile tab](images/palette-tile.webp) ![The Blend tab](images/palette-blend.webp)\n\n- **Rect** lays flat ground and nothing else, for filling an area you will shape\n  afterwards, or for ground under a doodad.\n- **Tile** is for the pieces the isometric brush will not give you: a single cliff tile, a\n  doodad's tile, a corner the cliff sets do not have.\n- **Blend** is for joins the cliff sets never had: dirt into a doodad's base, one edge set\n  into another, a hand-laid shoreline. Click a tile to make it the anchor, and the palette\n  lists for each side the tiles whose opposite edge continues it, best seam first.\n\nThese three leave the isometric record alone.\n\n### Ground a mod adds\n\nA mod's tileset can hold flat ground the game never had, and the tileset's files give that\nground no terrain type and nothing the isometric brush could join it with: Cosmonarchy's\nJungle has mud on high ground, a swamp, asphalt and more, all of it placed tile by tile in\nthe mod's own maps. The Isometric tab therefore lists only the game's terrain types. The\nRect tab lists the rest it can stand behind, below the game's:\n\n- **The game's ground at another height.** Where a pair of tile groups has exactly the\n  pictures of one of the game's terrains under other flags, it is listed under that\n  terrain's name and its height: *Mud (mid ground)*, *Mud (high ground)*. Mid ground is\n  the level of High Dirt. The Tile tab names those groups the same way, and its *Flat\n  terrain* filter includes them.\n- **Ground the open map lays.** A pair the map already uses as ground, left and right\n  halves side by side, is listed as *Group* and its number. Nothing in a tileset tells\n  flat ground from the pieces of a seam between two terrains of nearly one colour, so the\n  map is what the editor goes by.\n- **A pair you pick.** In the Tile tab, right-click and choose **Paint This Tile's Pair\n  with the Rect Brush** to lay any pair as ground, on a map that has none of it yet.\n  Alt+click on such ground in the map picks it too.\n\nAll of these paint, flood-fill and take part in **Tools ▸ Replace Terrain…** like the\ngame's terrains — replacing Mud with *Mud (mid ground)* raises a mud flat without changing\nhow it looks. The seams around them are the mod's own pieces: lay them with the Tile\nbrush, or let the Blend brush find the tiles that continue an edge.\n\n### Overlays and tools\n\nTwo overlays on the View menu show what the ground means to the game:\n\n![The elevation overlay tints each minitile by its height](images/terrain-elevation.webp)\n\n![The buildability overlay hatches the tiles a building cannot stand on](images/terrain-buildability.webp)\n\n**View ▸ Walkability** (Ctrl+Shift+W) draws the ground as a unit walks it: islands, the\nareas the map divides into, the chokes between them with their widths, and the distances\nbetween start locations. In the editor at scmjs.dev its panel (Tools ▸ Walkability…) can\nalso have the game itself walk the map: the regions the game plans routes over, real units\nwalked between the start locations, a group of twelve units timed through every choke, and\nhow fast each start location mines with 8, 16 and 24 workers.\n\n**File ▸ Import ▸ Terrain from Image…** turns a picture into terrain with the isometric\nbrush, over the whole map or a rectangle. Walkability and Terrain from Image are plugins,\non from the start; see [Plugins](#plugins).\n\n**Tools ▸ Replace Terrain…** turns every tile of one terrain type (or one exact tile) into\nanother, over the whole map or the area marked on the Cut / Copy / Paste layer. **Tools ▸\nFill Terrain** lays the whole map anew. Both are one undo step.\n\n**Symmetry** mirrors what you do across the map's axes or about its centre. `M` or the\ntoolbar button turns it on and off, and the list beside the button picks the mode: left ↔\nright, top ↔ bottom, four-way, a half turn, quarter turns, or either diagonal. The last\nthree need a square map. While a mode is on, the axes show on the map and the ghost shows\nwhere the copies will land.\n\n- The terrain brushes (all but Blend), the fills and the fog brush also paint on the\n  mirror images.\n- Placing a unit, sprite, doodad or location places one at each image as well. Each copy\n  goes to its own player: P1's start location gives P2's under the two-way modes, and\n  P2's, P3's and P4's under the four-way ones. Neutral units and resources stay neutral. A\n  doodad that would have to turn is skipped.\n- Selecting something also selects whatever stands at its images. Dragging then moves them\n  all as mirror images, resizing a location resizes its copies, and Delete removes them\n  all. A map made symmetric by hand works the same way.\n\nEach change is one undo step. **Tools ▸ Symmetry…** describes each mode and has the option\nto keep copies for the same player instead.\n\n![The Symmetry dialog](images/symmetry.webp)\n\nThe Terrain palette's *Remove stranded units* tick deletes the ground units a stroke has\njust left where they cannot walk, which the game would remove at the start, as part of the\nsame undo step. Buildings stay where they are: the game makes them on any ground.\n\n## Doodads\n\nDoodads are the trees, rocks, ruins, ramps and bridges of a map: pieces of terrain that\ncome with their own tiles and, sometimes, a sprite drawn over them. The palette is built\nfrom the current tileset's own groups, with StarEdit's categories and placement rules.\n\n![The Doodads layer on a Desert map, placing a sand dune](images/doodads.webp)\n\nPicking a doodad arms placement, and the ghost turns red where the footprint leaves the\nmap, covers another doodad or sits on the wrong terrain. *Place anywhere* drops that rule;\n*Snap to grid* keeps the footprint on StarEdit's two-tile isometric grid, for a move as\nwell as a placement. Right-click or Esc stops placing.\n\nThe three buttons beside the category list set how large the previews are drawn: small\nfits several to a row, large draws one per row so a doodad's detail can be read. The\nchoice is remembered.\n\nDoodads have no names in the game data, so the search box matches what the data does say:\n\n- the category: `bridge`, `temple`, `coastal`;\n- the id: `#12`;\n- the footprint: `6×6`;\n- the sprite or unit an overlay draws: `hdrock`, `Xel'Naga Temple`;\n- `ramp`, for anything whose tiles are ramp tiles (StarEdit files ramps under cliffs and\n  walls).\n\nThe search stays in the box when you switch to another layer and back, and the palette\nscrolls to the doodad you picked. Clearing the search (✕ or Esc) opens the picked doodad's\ncategory, so it stays in view; choosing a category from the list leaves the search and\nshows that category instead.\n\nA placed doodad stays whole (its tiles, its record and any canopy or door overlay), and\nremoving one restores the ground underneath. When you want the tiles without the doodad,\nsuch as a ramp to touch up piece by piece or a cliff edge to paint into, right-click it\nand choose *Convert Doodad to Terrain* (the Properties panel has the same button). The\ntiles stay, the doodad goes, and an overlay stays on as an ordinary sprite. From then on\nthe Terrain layer treats the cells as ground. *Place as terrain* in the palette does the\nsame at placement, for ramps and cliff pieces you mean to work over by hand.\n\n## Units\n\nThe Units layer places everything the game has a unit for: the three races' units and\nbuildings, heroes, critters, resources, start locations, beacons and powerups. The palette\ngroups them by race, and the search box takes a name or an id. With a data set made from a\nmod that has more unit types than the game, the extra types appear under a heading named\nfor the data set, by race and buildings; see [Data sets made from a\nmod](game-data.md#data-sets-made-from-a-mod). A type of the game's that the mod renamed\ninto something else (a Zerg unit where the game has a Terran building) moves under that\nheading too, by the race and kind the mod gives it. The Trigger Editor's unit lists follow\nthe same groups.\n\n![The Units layer: marines placed, and a tank refused because it overlaps them](images/units.webp)\n\nPicking a unit type arms placement, and each click on empty ground places one for the\nplayer selected at the top of the palette. Esc or right-click stops placing and drops you\ninto select mode, where a click selects, a drag on empty ground marks a box, and a drag on\na unit moves it. Del deletes the selection.\n\n**Snap to grid** puts buildings, resources, start locations and beacons on the tile grid\nby their placement box, the way StarEdit stores them, and every other unit on the nearest\ntile centre. Turn it off for pixel placement. It applies to a move as well as a placement,\nso dragging a unit that sits off the grid brings it back onto it.\n\nThe **placement checks** are on by default and refuse a spot with a red ghost and a reason\nin the status bar. They are the rules the game applies when it loads a map, silently\ndropping the units that do not fit:\n\n- no overlapping collision boxes for ground units and buildings;\n- buildable tiles under a building's whole placement box;\n- walkable ground under a unit.\n\nThe unit in the way gets outlined. Untick the checks to stack units on purpose, and expect\nthe game to keep only the first.\n\n![Unit Properties](images/unit-properties.webp)\n\nDouble-click a unit for every field its record holds: owner, position, hit points,\nshields, energy, resources and hangar count with their \"used\" ticks, the special\nproperties (cloaked, burrowed, lifted off, hallucinated, invincible), and the related-unit\nlink an add-on or a Nydus Canal uses. The game links two units only when each names the\nother, so setting the related unit sets the other unit back to this one. With several\nunits selected, only the fields you touch are written to all of them, so a mineral line\ncan be set to one amount in one go.\n\nAn add-on placed in its spot beside its owner's building is linked to it, as StarEdit\ndoes, and so is a building placed where a free add-on of its owner stands. Nydus Canals\nare linked in pairs as they are placed: each one to its owner's last canal without an\nexit. A Refinery, Extractor or Assimilator starts with 5000 gas, as a geyser does; without\nan amount in its record it would have none in the game. Mineral fields and geysers are\nalways placed for Player 12, whatever owner the palette has, as StarEdit places them: a\nmelee game keeps no one else's.\n\n**Tools ▸ Auto-place Start Locations…** puts one start location per player on a ring or in\nthe corners, each moved to the nearest ground the placement checks accept, and selects\nthem so you can drag them where you want. For a melee map, the Melee Wizard plugin\n(Plugins ▸ Browse Plugins…) lays out symmetric start locations with mineral lines and\ngeysers at the distance the game mines fastest from; see [Plugins](#plugins).\n\n## Sprites\n\nThere are two kinds of sprite. Pure sprites are a graphic drawn where it stands with no\nunit behind it: tree canopies, markers, glows. Unit sprites are records the game turns\ninto a unit when the map loads, which is how StarEdit does Installation doors and traps.\n\n![The Sprites layer](images/sprites.webp)\n\nThe palette has a tab for each. Pure Sprites lists all 517 entries grouped as Units,\nEffects and Doodads, the last named after the graphics file since the game has no names\nfor them. Placement is free: click anywhere, no rules to refuse a spot. The *Disabled*\ntick is the sprite flag that starts a door or trap closed or switched off. Sprite\nProperties also shows the 0x4000 bit StarEdit writes on some doodad overlays; the game\ndoes not read it, and a sprite faces the way its own animation sets.\n\nDoodad overlays are ordinary sprites and can be selected here too. The Properties panel\nsays which doodad one belongs to, and warns that moving it alone leaves the doodad's tiles\nbehind.\n\n## Locations\n\nLocations are the rectangles that triggers refer to, as in \"bring a unit to *Beacon*\" or\n\"create a unit at *Spawn*\". A melee map needs none.\n\n![The Locations layer with two locations](images/locations.webp)\n\nDrag on empty ground to create one, drag the eight handles to resize, drag inside to move,\nand use the arrow keys to nudge the selected ones (with nothing selected they scroll the\nview). *Snap* is off, 8 or 16 pixels, one tile, or 64 pixels; a move snaps the box's\ncorner rather than the pointer, so a box picked up off the grid lands on it. The palette\nlists every location in use, and Scenario ▸ Locations… is the same list as a sortable\ntable.\n\n![Location Properties](images/location-properties.webp)\n\nDouble-click one for its name and exact bounds, and for the elevation ticks: a location\napplies only on the ticked elevations, which is how you keep a trigger from firing on the\ncliff above it. An amber stripe on a location's row marks one that excludes something.\n\nA location can be stored inverted, its right edge left of its left edge or its bottom\nabove its top; some maps do this on purpose, and the bounds in Location Properties can set\nit. The game finds no units inside such a location, so Bring and the other conditions and\nactions that look for units there see nothing, while a unit created there still appears at\nits centre. The map draws an inverted location with a dashed edge, and dragging one of its\nhandles makes it an ordinary location again.\n\nA map has a fixed number of location slots: 64 in StarCraft 1.00 maps, 255 in Hybrid and\nBrood War ones. A new location takes the lowest free slot, deleting one empties it, and\ntriggers keep pointing at the slot they named.\n\nThe 64th slot is **Anywhere** (the editor counts slots from 0, so it shows as 63). It is\nthe location every trigger can pick, and the game and other editors depend on it covering\nexactly the map. It is pinned at the top of the list with a lock, never drawn, never\npicked by a click, and cannot be moved, renamed or deleted. If a map's Anywhere has gone\nmissing or no longer matches the map, the editor offers to put it back.\n\nA map built with euddraft (eudplib's build) has no readable locations in the file.\neuddraft stores its loader in the location table, scrambled, and the game puts the real\nlocations back when the map starts. If drawn, those slots would cover the whole map, so\nthe editor hides locations when it opens such a map and says so in a notice. Its *Show*\nbutton or the Locations eye brings them back. The next map with real locations shows them\nagain, unless you have turned them on or off yourself in between.\n\n## Fog of war\n\nThe fog of war decides which tiles a player starts the game unable to see. Every player\nhas their own. A new map starts every tile fogged for every player, and that is what the\neditor draws until the first stroke. Only Use Map Settings games read the fog a map sets:\na melee game starts every player fully fogged whatever the map says.\n\n![The Fog of War layer at 50% zoom, clearing an area for Player 1](images/fog.webp)\n\nSelect any of the eight players at the top of the palette and every stroke and fill edits\nall of them at once; *View* picks whose fog the map and minimap show. *Fog* and *Clear*\nare the brush modes, Shift-drag paints the opposite, and Alt+click picks up which players\nhave fog on a tile. Fog All, Clear All, Invert and Copy Fog act on the whole map for the\nselected players.\n\nFogged tiles darken over everything, units and locations included, in the tint the game\nitself uses for that tileset.\n\n## Cut, copy and paste\n\nTo copy part of a map, drag on it with the Cut / Copy / Paste layer active (`C`) to mark a\nrectangle, then Ctrl+C or Ctrl+X. On the object layers the same keys act on the selection\ninstead, and the clip is the selection's bounding box carrying just those objects, so a\nbase can be copied from either side.\n\n![A marked area copied and being pasted](images/clipboard.webp)\n\n*Include* says both what a copy takes and what a paste lays down: terrain, doodads, units,\nsprites, locations, fog. Terrain carries the ground under the doodads too, so a paste\nwithout doodads shows plain ground rather than half a tree. Doodads are pasted with their\noverlays, units get fresh ids with add-on and Nydus links kept when both ends came along,\nand locations take free slots, which is the one thing a paste can run out of.\n\nCtrl+V arms the pointer with a ghost of the clip. A click stamps it and stays armed for\nthe next; Esc or right-click stops. *Merge* adds to what is there; *Replace* clears the\nunits, sprites and doodads under the clip first, though never locations. Terrain from a\ndifferent tileset is refused, since tile numbers mean something else there, while the\nobjects still paste. The clip survives File ▸ Open, which is how a base moves between\nmaps.\n\nA piece worth keeping for other maps, such as a ramp, a bridge or a cliff corner, can be\nsaved as a *stamp* (Edit ▸ Save as Stamp…, or the right-click menu on the marked area) and\nlaid down on any later map from Tools ▸ Stamp Library…; see [Plugins](#plugins).\n\n## Triggers\n\nTriggers turn a map into a scenario: *when* these conditions hold, *for* these players,\n*do* these actions. A Use Map Settings map is built from them. A melee game ignores the\nmap's triggers and applies its own rules, so a melee map needs none. (Blizzard's melee\nmaps carry the three standard triggers, for defeat, victory and starting resources, for\nanyone who plays them as Use Map Settings.)\n\nSeveral editors work on the same list of triggers, and you can switch between them freely:\n\n- **The Trigger Editor** (Ctrl+T): StarEdit's form.\n- **The Text Trigger Editor**: the triggers as text in SCMDraft 2's syntax. It is a\n  plugin, installed but off.\n- **TrigScript**: triggers written as code. See [TrigScript](#trigscript).\n- **Magenta** (Plugins ▸ Browse Plugins…): every trigger as a sentence, with a catalogue\n  of StarCraft: Remastered EUD conditions and actions.\n\nMission briefings have an editor of their own. The [trigger reference](triggers.md) has a\npage for every condition and action.\n\nTo see how a map's triggers fit together, the Trigger Map plugin (Plugins ▸ Browse\nPlugins…) draws them as a graph: which triggers set a switch, a death counter or a\nlocation and which ones read it, with a list of switches nothing sets, counters nothing\nreads and triggers that never run.\n\n### The Trigger Editor\n\nCtrl+T, or Triggers ▸ Trigger Editor…. A player filter on the left, the trigger list in\nthe middle, and for the selected trigger its players, up to 16 conditions and up to 64\nactions.\n\n![The Trigger Editor on Big Game Hunters' three melee triggers](images/trigger-editor.webp)\n\nThe Players tab offers the owners the game hands triggers to: Players 1–8, All Players and\nthe four forces. A trigger ticked only for other groups never runs, so those groups\n(Player 9–12, Current Player, Foes, Allies and the rest, which are arguments rather than\nowners) only show when a trigger already has one ticked, so that it can be cleared.\n\nEvery condition and action the game has is there, except that *Mission Briefing* is\noffered only in briefings, since in a trigger it is never true. Each argument gets a\ncontrol to suit it (a player pick, a unit type list, a location list, a number, a text box\nwith the colour codes), and any value the lists do not include can still be typed as a raw\nnumber, which is how EUD values and unusual unit ids get in. An item can be disabled\nwithout deleting it; a disabled one is kept in the file and skipped by the game. *Preserve\ntrigger* is the flag that keeps a trigger firing more than once.\n\nA new *Create Unit*, *Create Unit with Properties* or *Set Deaths* starts on Terran Marine\nrather than *Any unit*: the game creates nothing for a unit class, and Set Deaths on *Any\nunit* writes nothing. A Create Unit amount of 0 makes one unit, where elsewhere 0 means\nall of them. The unit list of the two Create Unit actions holds only what the game will\ncreate, the units of StarEdit's palette; larvae, eggs, traps, doors and the like are left\nout, because the game refuses them.\n\n### The Text Trigger Editor\n\nThe same triggers as text, in SCMDraft 2's TrigEdit syntax, so text from SCMDraft pastes\nin and text from here pastes back out. Compile checks it and reports the first line that\ndoes not parse.\n\n![The Text Trigger Editor](images/text-triggers.webp)\n\nIt is the [TrigEdit](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-trigedit) plugin, installed but\nstarting *off*: tick it on in Plugins ▸ Manage Plugins…, and Triggers ▸ Text Trigger\nEditor… and its Ctrl+Shift+T appear.\n\nA leading `;` disables a line, and a `Flags:` block carries the trigger flags SCMDraft has\nno syntax for. The *Briefing* switch at the top edits the mission briefing in the same\nsyntax.\n\n### Mission briefings\n\nTriggers ▸ Mission Briefing Editor… (also Scenario ▸ Mission Briefing…) is the same form\nover the briefing actions: text, portraits, transmissions and the pauses between them, one\nbriefing per player. The layout matches the briefings on Blizzard's own multiplayer maps.\n\n![The Mission Briefing editor on Ground Zero](images/briefing.webp)\n\n### Unit properties slots\n\nTriggers ▸ Unit Properties Slots… is the table behind the *Create Unit with Properties*\naction: 64 slots, each the hit points, shields and energy (as percentages), resources,\nhangar count and special states a trigger applies to the units it creates. The action's\nargument lists the slots by what they set and opens this editor on one.\n\n![The Unit Properties Slots dialog](images/cuwp.webp)\n\n### EUD\n\nAn EUD trigger uses a player or unit value outside the game's tables to reach other parts\nof the game's memory ([EUD](triggers.md#eud) in the trigger reference has the details).\nFor this, the player pick next to a Deaths condition or Set Deaths action has an *EPD*\nbox: type a memory address and it becomes the player value that reaches it through the\ndeaths table, and a raw value shows which address it reaches. Check Map points out the raw\nvalues so they are not mistaken for errors.\n\nFor named reads and writes of the game (a unit's hit points, a player's minerals), use a\n[TrigScript program](trigscript.md#programs) or Magenta. Both make maps that play on\nStarCraft: Remastered only.\n\n### Importing, exporting and checking triggers\n\n**File ▸ Import** and **File ▸ Export** (also Triggers ▸ Import Triggers… and Export\nTriggers…) carry triggers as `.trg` files, SCMDraft's format, or as text. An import can be\nadded to the map's triggers or replace them. A `.trg` holds string numbers rather than\ntext, so it fits only a copy of the same map; text works between maps. From a terminal,\n`scmjs map triggers export` and `import` do the same ([Triggers and strings as\ntext](cli.md#triggers-and-strings-as-text)).\n\n**Triggers ▸ Validate Triggers** is [Check Map](#check-map) limited to the triggers.\n\n## TrigScript\n\n![The TrigScript editor on a wave-defence script: the files on the left and the code checked as you type](images/trigscript.webp)\n\nTrigScript writes triggers as code: TypeScript files kept inside the map. It is a plugin,\non from the start, and Triggers ▸ TrigScript… opens it once a map is open. Each\n`trigger()` call the script makes becomes an ordinary trigger, the same kind the Trigger\nEditor shows, and plays on any version of the game. Code inside `program(() => { … })`\nruns in the game itself, with variables, arrays, functions and loops, and **a map with a\nprogram in it needs StarCraft: Remastered**.\n\nTrigScript has [a guide of its own](trigscript.md):\n\n- [Quick start](trigscript.md#quick-start): a first script, from opening the window to the\n  triggers it makes.\n- [Writing triggers](trigscript.md#writing-triggers): `trigger()` and the helpers for\n  writing many at once.\n- [Programs](trigscript.md#programs): code that runs in the game. Its language and its\n  library of modules have guides of their own: [TrigScript\n  language](trigscript-language.md) and [TrigScript library](trigscript-library.md).\n- [Coming from eudplib](trigscript.md#coming-from-eudplib): where each eudplib and\n  epScript idea went, for map makers who build EUD maps with euddraft.\n- [Testing and Simulate](trigscript.md#testing-and-simulate): trying the script in the\n  simulator, and tests that run after every change.\n- [The workspace](trigscript.md#the-workspace): the script window, its files, and the\n  script in a panel beside the map.\n- [Reference](trigscript.md#reference): every function, and what is not supported.\n\n## Scenario settings\n\nThe Scenario menu holds the map's own tables. Nothing in a dialog changes the map until OK\nor Apply, and what an OK or Apply wrote is one undo step: Ctrl+Z after closing the dialog\ntakes the whole of it back, and Ctrl+Y puts it on again.\n\n### Map Properties, Players, Forces, Colours\n\n![Map Properties](images/map-properties.webp)\n\n**Map Properties** (Alt+Enter) is the name and description the lobby shows, the tileset,\nthe size and the revision, with buttons to the rest.\n\nChanging the tileset lays the terrain again with a terrain you pick — tile numbers mean\nsomething else in every tileset — and drops the doodads, while units, sprites, locations,\nfog and triggers stay. You can also keep the tile numbers, to see what they draw in the\nnew tileset. **Resize / Crop Map…** grows or trims the map about a corner or the centre.\nEach is one undo step: Ctrl+Z puts back the old size or tileset, with the terrain, doodads\nand units the change removed. (On a shared map, both clear everyone's undo history\ninstead.)\n\n![Player Settings](images/player-settings.webp)\n\n**Player Settings** is each slot's controller (Human, Computer, Rescuable, Neutral…),\nrace, colour and force. Under a mod's data set the race list also has the races the mod\nadds, when they are named ([Races a data set\nadds](game-data.md#a-map-remembers-its-data-set)); a race the map holds that nothing names\nshows as *Race* and its number and is kept as it is.\n\n![Force Settings](images/force-settings.webp)\n\n**Force Settings** groups the players into up to four teams and sets, per team, allied\nvictory, shared vision, allies and random start locations.\n\n**Player Colors** picks each slot's palette colour and, for Remastered, a custom RGB,\nrandom, or the player's own choice; the map repaints as you change them. What the game\nshows depends on the map's revision:\n\n- **Brood War and Remastered 1.21+**: the game reads the palette colours. Only the first\n  twelve work as a palette colour: for the rest StarCraft 1.16.1 shows a wrong colour and\n  Remastered the player's usual one, and Tools ▸ Check Map says so.\n- **StarCraft 1.00, Hybrid and Remastered without Brood War**: StarCraft 1.16.1 shows\n  every player in their slot's usual colour, whatever the map says. Remastered takes the\n  colours from its own colour table, which the editor writes whenever a colour is not the\n  slot's own.\n\nIn Remastered, a custom RGB gives the player any colour, including the ten past the first\ntwelve. Random and the player's own choice both show as Random in the lobby; with the\nplayer's own choice, it is a list the player can change. A player left on Random gets a\ndifferent colour each game.\n\n![Player Colors](images/player-colors.webp)\n\n### Units, upgrades and technology\n\n![Unit Settings](images/unit-settings.webp)\n\n**Unit Settings**, **Upgrade Settings** and **Technology Settings** edit the cost and\navailability tables:\n\n- a unit's hit points, armour, build time, cost and weapon damage, a custom name, and\n  which players may build it;\n- an upgrade's cost and levels;\n- a technology's cost, and whether each player starts with it or may research it.\n\nA row on *use default* shows the game's own numbers greyed out, and starts from them when\nyou untick it.\n\nA map's settings cover the game's 228 unit types, 61 upgrades and 44 technologies; that\nsize is fixed by the map format. A unit type, upgrade or technology a mod's data set adds\npast those is listed in its dialog, which says it has no per-map settings ([Data sets made\nfrom a mod](game-data.md#data-sets-made-from-a-mod)). Upgrades and technologies are named\nas the mod's own lists name them where it has them (`arr\\upgrades.txt` and\n`arr\\techdata.txt`).\n\n### Strings\n\n![The String Editor](images/string-editor.webp)\n\nEvery piece of text a map carries (its name, the force names, location names, every\ntrigger message) is a numbered string, and the **String Editor** lists them all with where\neach is used. Edits keep their number, so triggers and locations keep pointing where they\ndid; *Delete unused* clears out what nothing refers to.\n\nThe row of buttons inserts the game's colour codes, each drawn in the colour it produces,\nand the preview under the box shows the string the way the game draws it. The same codes\nwork in every field whose text the game draws: the map name and description, force names,\ncustom unit names, and the text of a trigger or briefing action. Each of those fields\nshows the string as the game draws it until you click into it, and has a palette button at\nits right-hand end.\n\nThe previews follow Remastered's rule, where a colour set on one line carries onto the\nnext. StarCraft 1.16.1 reset the colour at every line break, so an older map's text can\nshow in colours its author did not choose. The *1.16.1 colours* tick (also in Preferences\n▸ View) switches every preview to the old rule, and the Repair plugin offers to write the\nresets the old game supplied. Two codes do something you might not expect, in both games:\n`<01>` goes back to the colour before the last change rather than to the default, and once\nthe text is grey (`<05>`) the game ignores every later colour code in the string. The\npreviews follow both.\n\n**File ▸ Export ▸ Strings (.txt)…** writes every string to a numbered text file, and\n**File ▸ Import ▸ Strings (.txt)…** reads such a file back, replacing the strings by\nnumber. That is a way to translate or proofread a map's text outside the editor.\n\n**Text that looks garbled.** If a map's text opens as nonsense, often Korean or Japanese\ntext in an older map, the editor guessed its encoding wrong. A map file does not say what\nencoding its text is in, so the editor guesses from the bytes when the map opens. Open\n**Scenario ▸ Map Revision…**, choose the right encoding (*Korean*, say), and every string\nis fixed at once. The same choice decides how the text is written on save, so a map made\nfor 1.16.1 on a Korean Windows stays readable there. A new map is UTF-8, which Remastered\nreads, and a map moved to Remastered's string table becomes UTF-8 too. Western text saved\nas Windows-1252 can go wrong in Remastered, which reads some of it as Korean: \"Ärger\nDon’t\" shows as \"훣ger Don뭪\" (see [the string table](chk-format.md#str)). Text saved as\nUTF-8 always shows as written. A character the chosen encoding cannot hold is saved as\n`?`; Check Map and the Save dialog say so beforehand.\n\n### Sounds and switches\n\nThe **Sound Editor** lists the map's sounds with the `.wav` files in the archive: import,\nplay, remove, and adopt files the archive carries but the list does not. Import takes any\nfile the browser can decode (MP3, FLAC, AAC, Ogg, WAV in any encoding) and writes it as a\nWAV in the format picked next to the button. The default, 22050 Hz 16-bit mono, is what\nthe game's own sounds are; the smaller presets are for maps near the size limit. A *Play\nWAV* action then names the file. Some maps made for Remastered carry Ogg sounds instead;\nRemastered plays them in a map of any revision, while StarCraft 1.16.1 stays silent, and\nCheck Map points them out.\n\nA trigger can also play one of the game's own sounds, a unit's voice or an interface\nclick, without the file being in the map. In the Trigger Editor, **Browse…** beside a WAV\nargument lists the map's sounds and the game's together. Search by file name, by unit, or\nby when the unit says the line: \"marine yes\" finds the four things a Marine says when\nordered. In a Transmission the list opens on the lines of the unit the action already\nnames. The line under the field says what the path is, and warns when it is neither in the\nmap nor one of the game's. The list does not play the sounds.\n\n**Switches** names the 256 switches triggers set and test, so the trigger editors show\n\"Gate open\" rather than \"Switch 3\".\n\n## Checking, testing and saving\n\nBefore a map is played, it can be checked for common mistakes, tried in the game, and\nsaved in the form you want to release.\n\n### Check Map\n\n![Check Map on a new map: eight players and no start locations yet](images/check-map.webp)\n\nTools ▸ Check Map… looks for what the game would refuse or silently drop. A map with no\ntriggers is judged as a melee map and one with triggers as a scenario, since that is how\neach is played.\n\n- **The file:** missing sections, and any section whose length the game does not accept,\n  with what the player would see (*Invalid Scenario* in the map list, *Error loading\n  scenario file* as a game starts, in every game type or only in Use Map Settings, a\n  briefing that does not play); a revision the game does not know; a map side longer than\n  256; a Brood War tileset on a revision without Brood War, and an Installation map with\n  no triggers, which the game plays only as Use Map Settings. A verification table\n  (`VCOD`) that fails the game's check for the map's players is reported, and Save puts\n  StarEdit's table, which always passes, in its place.\n- **Players and start locations:** a player without a start location, which eliminates\n  them at once in melee and only moves the opening view in a scenario; several start\n  locations for one player (the game uses the last); start locations for Players 9–12;\n  player colours on a StarCraft 1.00 or Hybrid map, which StarCraft 1.16.1 does not show,\n  and colours past the twelve the game takes from the palette.\n- **Units the game leaves out:** in a scenario, the placed units of a player whose race is\n  User Selectable (the game removes them and hands out melee start units instead) and of a\n  slot that is not in the game; in melee, everything but start locations and Player 12's\n  resources and critters; ground units standing where they cannot walk; resource amounts\n  past 65,535, which the game keeps as 16 bits; more than 1700 units, or units off the\n  map.\n- **Text:** a string table past STR's 64 KB of offsets (Save refuses it), or a Remastered\n  string table in a revision that does not read it; a map name or force name longer than\n  the lobby shows (Remastered counts the name's bytes as UTF-8, where a Korean syllable\n  takes three), and a description longer than StarCraft 1.16.1 shows; UTF-8 text a 1.16.1\n  player sees garbled; Windows-1252 text Remastered shows as Korean; a custom unit name on\n  a type that uses the default settings (the game ignores it).\n- **Triggers:** a missing or moved Anywhere location; locations that are unused or not set\n  at all (a location action without one does nothing); strings that do not exist; unit and\n  player values the game does not have; condition and action types it does not have, which\n  crash StarCraft 1.16.1 (Remastered takes such a condition as false and skips such an\n  action); Create Unit, Set Resources and Set Score for Players 9–12, which do nothing;\n  Create Unit of a unit class or of a type StarEdit's palette leaves out, which makes\n  nothing; a trigger ticked twice for one player, which runs twice; a trigger with flag\n  0x02, which defeats every other player each time its actions run; Mission Briefing in a\n  trigger, and a briefing that does not start with it; AI scripts a trigger owned only by\n  people, rescuable or neutral slots cannot run; Play WAV actions with no file in the map,\n  and Ogg sounds, which only Remastered plays; switches tested for Set but never set.\n- **Terrain:** tiles with bit 15 set, which the game files as a creep flag, though in play\n  such a tile shows no creep and takes no Zerg building; tiles from groups the tileset\n  does not have; tiles only Remastered's tilesets have; tiles from terrain neither\n  StarCraft 1.16.1 nor Remastered has, which means the map was made for a mod (not listed\n  while that mod's data set is in use); a missing or outdated isometric record.\n\nA map with triggers gets three more checks:\n\n- a human player with no way to win or lose: no Victory, and no Defeat or win for another\n  player (in a scenario nobody wins or loses unless a trigger says so);\n- no Set Mission Objectives;\n- with hyper triggers present, a Wait or a person's Transmission in another trigger of the\n  same player, which fights them for the player's one wait slot (see\n  [Waits](triggers.md#waits) in the trigger reference).\n\nDouble-click an issue to go to it. The same check runs inside the Save dialog.\n\nFrom a terminal, `scmjs map check` runs the same check over any number of maps, and can\nfail a CI build on it ([Checking a map](cli.md#checking-a-map)).\n\n### Test Map\n\n![The Test Map dialog](images/test-map.webp)\n\nTools ▸ Test Map (Ctrl+F5, or the toolbar's Test button) hands the map to the game. The\ndesktop app has one place to send it: StarCraft. The editor at scmjs.dev has two,\nStarCraft and the browser, and **Send the map to** at the top of **Edit ▸ Preferences ▸\nTesting** chooses which one Test Map uses. It is the browser until you choose. The Tools\nmenu also has **Test in StarCraft** and **Test in Browser**, which go to that place\nwhatever the choice. **Tools ▸ Test Map Settings…** opens that Preferences page.\n\n**StarCraft.** The game cannot open a map from the outside, so the editor writes it into a\n`scmJS` folder under the game's Maps folder, where Single Player ▸ Custom Game lists it.\n\n- **The desktop app** finds the installation (or takes a folder you pick) and starts the\n  game as well.\n- **Chrome and Edge** write the map into a folder you pick once. Start the game yourself\n  and open the map under Single Player ▸ Custom Game.\n- **Firefox and Safari** download the map; move it into the game's Maps folder.\n\nThe folder, and in the desktop app whether the game starts, are under StarCraft on Edit ▸\nPreferences ▸ Testing. The first test in a browser with no folder yet opens a dialog to\npick one.\n\n**The browser.** In the editor at scmjs.dev, **Tools ▸ Test in Browser** opens the map on\nthe site's play page, scmjs.dev/play, which runs the game in a browser tab. The map goes\nstraight from the editor's tab to that one, and testing again sends the new version to the\nsame tab. The game runs from your own StarCraft files: the first time, the play page asks\nfor the folder StarCraft is installed in. Blizzard's map editor download is not enough for\nit.\n\nHow the map is played is set under *This browser* on **Edit ▸ Preferences ▸ Testing**:\n\n- **Game type.** *From the map* (the default) plays with the map's own settings when it\n  has computer players or units placed beyond the start locations, and as a melee game\n  otherwise. *Use Map Settings* and *Melee* always play that way. *Choose on the play\n  page* opens the map on the play page's setup screen, which lists the map's forces and\n  slots as the game's own lobby does, and nothing starts until you press Start Game.\n- **Your race**, **your slot** for Use Map Settings (the first open one, or a player you\n  name), and for melee the number of **computers** and their race. Where the map fixes a\n  slot's race or has fewer places, the map's settings win.\n\n**Keeping the game.** **Save Replay** in the play page's Game Menu saves the game you are\nplaying as a replay file, as far as it has been played. When the game ends, the play page\noffers it again beside **Watch the Replay**, **Analyse in the Editor**, which opens the\nreplay in a new editor tab with Aftermath, and **Play Again**.\n\n**Debugging triggers.** **Tools ▸ Debug Triggers in Browser** plays the map in a panel\nunder the map instead of a tab, always with the map's own settings, and shows what its\ntriggers do while you play:\n\n- **Pause**, **Step Frame**, **Next Pass** and **Previous Pass**. A *pass* is one time the\n  game runs the triggers: every 31 frames, or more often while Waits are running out.\n  Going back replays your own orders, so stepping forward again reaches the same game;\n  giving an order after going back plays on from there instead.\n- A timeline with a column for each pass: how many triggers fired (green), were held at an\n  action such as a Wait (amber), or stopped at a condition (grey). Click a column to see\n  that pass.\n- The triggers of the selected pass, each with what happened to it: *fired*, *held at\n  action 2: Wait*, *condition 2 did not hold: Deaths*. The game stops checking a trigger's\n  conditions at the first that does not hold, so that is the one named. Click a trigger to\n  open it in the Trigger Editor. Triggers a build step added, such as a TrigScript\n  program's, are listed but have no row of their own to open.\n\nRun the command again to rebuild the map and start over in the same panel.\n\n> **Note.** Testing and debugging in the browser come with the scm-sim plugin and appear\n> only in the editor at scmjs.dev, not in the desktop app or an editor hosted elsewhere.\n> There, Test Map goes to StarCraft and Preferences ▸ Testing has no choice to make.\n\n**A map for a mod.** While a mod's data set is in use (see [Data sets made from a\nmod](game-data.md#data-sets-made-from-a-mod)), the map Test Map builds carries it, so the\nplay page knows which mod the map was made for. StarCraft, started on its own, plays the\ngame's own data rather than the mod's, and the dialog says so; so does a target that\ncannot take the data set.\n\nFrom a terminal, `scmjs map trace` runs a map's triggers in the game and lists which\nfired, and `scmjs map test` plays scenarios you write and checks how each ended ([Running\na map in the game](cli.md#running-a-map-in-the-game)).\n\n### Saving\n\nCtrl+S writes the map back where it came from.\n\n- **Chrome, Edge and the desktop app** keep a handle to the file you opened, so Save\n  writes in place once the browser has asked for permission.\n- **Firefox and Safari** give the editor a file's contents but no way to write it back, so\n  every save is a download, and the notice at the bottom right says so.\n\nSave As (Ctrl+Shift+S) and Save Copy As… open the Save dialog; a copy is written without\nthe open map changing its name or file.\n\n![The Save dialog](images/save.webp)\n\nThe Save dialog asks for a file name, a format and what to keep. Everything else is folded\naway underneath and can be left alone:\n\n- **Format**: `.scx` (Brood War), `.scm` (original StarCraft), or a bare `.chk`, the\n  scenario alone with no archive around it.\n- **What to keep**: *Everything* writes the whole file, so any editor can open it with\n  nothing lost. *Smallest that plays* leaves out the parts only an editor reads (the\n  isometric record, the editor's copies of the player settings and so on) and compresses\n  the archive as StarEdit does; the map plays the same, but an editor opening the file\n  later has less to work with. *Custom* shows each of those parts with its own tick. The\n  map in the editor is not changed either way.\n- **Archive** (folded): the compression, StarEdit's encryption tick, and the other files\n  in the archive. PKWARE is what StarEdit writes, and every StarCraft version reads it;\n  zlib is smaller but only Remastered reads it (StarCraft 1.16.1 cannot open such a map);\n  none is the largest and readable by anything. A map keeps the compression it was opened\n  with. The sounds and the files plugins keep in the archive each have a tick, so a copy\n  for release can leave them out. Files in the archive the editor cannot name, which are\n  common in protected maps, are kept exactly as they were, and the fold says how many\n  there are.\n- **Sections** (folded): every section the file will hold, with its size and whether it\n  changed, was left out or was merged.\n\nIf Check Map finds problems, the dialog says how many beside a button that opens it; the\nmap saves either way. The options confirmed here are what Ctrl+S reuses for that map from\nthen on.\n\n**The file a save replaces** is kept, so a bad save can be undone:\n\n- **The desktop app** copies the old file to a `.bak` beside it first (`Lagoon.scx` to\n  `Lagoon.scx.bak`, replacing an older `.bak`). If the `.bak` cannot be written (a\n  read-only folder, say), the save still goes through, a notice says why, and the old file\n  goes to Previous Versions instead.\n- **Chrome and Edge** cannot put a file beside another, so the version replaced is kept in\n  the browser's storage, the last three of each file name. **File ▸ Previous Versions…**\n  lists them: **Open** puts one in its own tab with \"(previous)\" added to its name, and\n  **Save As** writes it out as it was.\n- **Firefox and Safari** download every save and replace nothing, so there is nothing to\n  keep.\n\nPreferences ▸ General ▸ Saving turns this off.\n\n**Maps built on save.** A map with a TrigScript program is built by TrigScript as the file\nis written. The file you save is the one the game plays. It also carries the map as you\nsee it in the editor, and that is what comes back when you open the file again. A notice\nshows while the build runs, with a button to save without waiting for it. If the build\nfails, the map is still saved and the notice says what went wrong; the file lacks the\nbuilt part until a later save succeeds. Test Map builds the same way, and stops on a\nfailure instead.\n\n**Save to scmjs.dev…**, under File and under Account, keeps a copy of the map on your\nscmjs.dev account as a numbered revision with a note. It does not change where Ctrl+S\nwrites. See [Keeping maps on scmjs.dev](#keeping-maps-on-scmjsdev).\n\nTo see what changed between two saved versions of a map, `scmjs map diff` compares them\nfrom a terminal and `--picture` draws where; [Maps in git](cli.md#maps-in-git) covers\nkeeping maps in git.\n\n### Recovery copies\n\nWhile a map has unsaved changes, the editor keeps a copy of it in the browser's storage\n(the app's own storage in the desktop app): every two minutes, and whenever the editor\ngoes to the background (another tab, the window minimised). The copy is removed as soon as\nthe map is saved or closed, so a copy only survives when the editor closed with work\nunsaved: a crash, a closed tab, a power cut.\n\nAt the next start the **Recover Maps** dialog lists what was left, with the map's name,\nits file, its size and when it was copied. **Restore** opens the map in its own tab with\nits changes unsaved, under its old file name, so Ctrl+S writes back to the same file\n(after the browser asks again for permission). **Later** keeps the copies for the next\nstart, and File ▸ Recover Maps… lists them at any time. Maps still open in another tab or\nwindow are never listed there.\n\nQuitting the desktop app and answering Don't Save removes the copies. A copy only covers\nchanges up to the moment it was made, and it lives in this browser: it is not a backup of\nthe file, and clearing the site's data removes it. Preferences ▸ General ▸ Recovery turns\nthe copies off or changes how often they are made.\n\n### Export an image\n\n![The Export Image dialog](images/export-image.webp)\n\nFile ▸ Export ▸ Image (.png)… renders the whole map to a PNG, with one setting that\ndecides what the picture is: at 32 pixels per tile it is the full game art, at 8 or 16 the\nsame smaller, at 4 and 2 the average tile colours with units as minimap dots, and at 1\npixel per tile it is the game's minimap. Units, locations, fog and the grid are each a\ntick.\n\nTo show how a map was made rather than how it ended up, the Timelapse plugin (Plugins ▸\nBrowse Plugins…) records the map one change at a time and exports the recording as a GIF\nor a WebM video; see [Plugins](#plugins).\n\nTools ▸ Statistics… counts what the map holds (units per player, resources, doodads,\ntriggers, strings), and Ctrl+F finds a unit, location, sprite, string or trigger by name.\n\n## Replays\n\nThe editor opens StarCraft replays (`.rep` files) and shows, on the map the game was\nplayed on, what the players did: where buildings went down and when, where units were\nsent, each player's build order and actions per minute, and the chat, on a timeline you\ncan scrub or play. Map makers use it to see which paths and chokes players use, where and\nwhen they expand, and whether one start location plays differently from another.\n\nReplays come with the [Aftermath](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-aftermath) plugin,\nwhich is installed and on from the start. To open one:\n\n- **File ▸ Open Replay…** picks one or more replays;\n- **File ▸ Open…** takes a replay as well as a map;\n- dropping replays anywhere on the editor opens them.\n\nReplays from every version of the game are read. **View ▸ Aftermath** opens Aftermath's\npanel with no replay in front, to drop replays on.\n\n### A replay's tab\n\nEach replay opens the map it was played on in a tab of its own. A replay opens view-only:\nits tab has an eye where the unsaved-changes dot would be, and the status bar says *View\nonly*. You can scroll, zoom, switch layers and select things, but nothing can be changed\nor saved:\n\n- the palette, Layers and Properties panels are hidden (the View menu has the layer\n  switches);\n- the tools do nothing, and every menu item and dialog that would change the map is\n  greyed;\n- the toolbar leaves out Save, Undo and Redo, Cut and Paste, the brush, Symmetry, Players\n  and Triggers, and has an **Edit a Copy** button in place of Save.\n\nSave Copy As… still works. **Edit a Copy** (the button, or File ▸ Edit a Copy) opens an\nordinary copy of the map in a new tab, which asks where to save the first time you save\nit.\n\nSwitching tabs switches replays, each keeping its own place on the timeline, and closing\nthe tab closes the replay.\n\n### The timeline and the panel\n\nWhile a replay's tab is in front, a timeline runs under the map: Play and Pause, the time\nand the speed, a graph of the game, and marks for the fights, the chat and players\nleaving. Space plays and pauses, ← and → go ten seconds back or on (a minute with Shift),\nand Home and End go to the start and the end.\n\nThe column on the right has the game's details, the players, and tabs for the build order,\nthe chat and the rest. On the map, each player's buildings and orders are drawn in their\ncolour as the timeline moves. The [Aftermath\nREADME](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-aftermath#use) describes every part.\n\n### The Replay view\n\nIn the editor at scmjs.dev, a replay's tab opens on the **Replay**: the game itself\nreplaying in the map's place, from the game's files you installed. Blizzard's map editor\ndownload is enough for this. A switch in the top-left corner goes between the Replay and\nthe **Map**, the overhead view with Aftermath's marks and heat maps. The timeline drives\nwhichever is shown.\n\nWhile the Replay is shown, the editor keeps what still makes sense there:\n\n- zoom works on it: View ▸ Zoom In and Zoom Out, Zoom to Fit, and the toolbar's\n  magnifiers;\n- the grid, the locations and other plugins' overlays are drawn over it, and View ▸ Grid,\n  Locations and Location Names turn them on and off;\n- View ▸ Fog of War shows the game's own fog, through the eyes picked in the **Vision**\n  menu under the replay: everyone, or one player;\n- the status bar's tile and position follow the pointer, and the minimap shows where the\n  game looks; press or drag on it to move the game's camera;\n- pointing at a unit shows what the game's console would: its name, owner, hit points and\n  what it is doing.\n\nWhatever applies only to the map behind it is left off the toolbar and greyed in the\nmenus: the layers, selecting, the clipboard, Find, the brush and the map's own toggles\nsuch as Units or Elevation.\n\n### Analysis\n\nIn the editor at scmjs.dev, every replay you open is also analysed: the game is played\nthrough in the background to see what really happened rather than what was ordered. Once\nit is done, Aftermath shows the units where they stood, what each player really built and\nlost, the fights and the key moments, and a camera (*Spectate*) that follows the action.\n**View ▸ Replay Analysis** opens a larger window with each player's economy and tech over\nthe game.\n\nOpen several replays of one map and **Map stats** takes them together: how each start\nlocation did, which bases were taken and when, where the fights happened, and the ground\nno unit ever entered.\n\n### Play From Here\n\nIn the editor at scmjs.dev, **Play From Here** on the replay's timeline opens the replay\non the play page at the moment shown, seen as the player picked in the **Vision** menu.\nThe game stops there. Take one of the players and play on alone, against a community bot,\nor with others who join by an invite. Going back to an earlier moment watches the replay\nagain.\n\nOnce you have taken a player, **Save Replay** in the Game Menu saves the whole game as one\nreplay: the original up to your moment, and what you played after it. **Try Again From\nHere** in the Watch Replay menu goes back to the moment you took over, with the same bot\non the other side, and the end of the game offers it too.\n\n> **Note.** In the desktop app and an editor hosted elsewhere, Aftermath works from the\n> players' orders alone: there is no Replay view and no analysis.\n\n### Replays of a mod\n\nA replay saved on the play page from a game of a mod names the mod. Aftermath then shows\n*Replay of a mod* with the mod's name, its hash, its makers' link and the plugins that\nran. In the editor at scmjs.dev it plays the replay with the copy of the mod your browser\nkeeps, the one added on the play page (Game Menu ▸ Mods ▸ Add a Mod…, see [the mods\nguide](mods.md#on-the-play-page)):\n\n- the Replay view plays the game with the mod, its plugins running where they ran;\n- the analysis plays it the same way, so the Game, Analysis and Cast tabs fill as for any\n  replay;\n- Play From Here opens the play page with the mod.\n\nWhen the browser has not got the mod, or keeps another version of it, the Replay view and\nthe analysis say so, with the mod's hash and its makers' link, and play nothing.\n\nThe replay's map itself is drawn from the editor's data set, like any map. When that data\nset has not got the mod's terrain, those tiles are hatched and the editor says so, with a\nbutton to draw from the mod's folder ([Terrain the data set has not\ngot](game-data.md#terrain-the-data-set-has-not-got)).\n\nNames and costs come from the data set the editor draws with. Add the mod as a data set\n(Help ▸ Game Data… ▸ Add a data set…, see [Data sets made from a\nmod](game-data.md#data-sets-made-from-a-mod)), or press **Attach the Mod's Folder…** in\nAftermath's panel, and units, upgrades and technologies are named by the mod. Until then\nthey show by number.\n\nA mod whose plugins run plays at about the game's own pace, which shows in two places:\n\n- the analysis takes over ten minutes for a twenty-minute game;\n- going ahead on the timeline to a part the game has not played yet plays forward to the\n  moment, and the timeline says how far it has got. Going back is quick: the game returns\n  to a moment kept on the way. A drag along the timeline moves the game once, when the\n  drag ends.\n\n## Plugins\n\nPlugins add tools to the editor, and several of the tools in this guide are plugins. Most\ncome installed and on; a few more are a click away in **Plugins ▸ Browse Plugins…**.\n\n| Plugin | Starts | Where | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [Aftermath](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-aftermath) | On | File ▸ Open Replay… | Opens replays over their map; see [Replays](#replays) |\n| [Paint](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-paint) | On | Tools ▸ Paint… (Ctrl+Shift+P) | Lines, shapes, spray and text, from the palette's pick |\n| [Repair](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-repair) | On | Tools ▸ Repair Map… | Repairs damaged and protected maps as they open |\n| [scm-sim](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-scm-sim) | On | Tools ▸ Test in Browser, Debug Triggers in Browser | Plays the map on scmjs.dev/play, or under the map with its triggers shown |\n| [scmjs.dev](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-scmjs-dev) | On | Account menu | Your [scmjs.dev account](#your-scmjsdev-account) |\n| [scmscx.com](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-scm-scx) | On | File ▸ Find on scmscx.com… | Searches the scmscx.com map archive |\n| [Stamp Library](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-stamp-library) | On | Tools ▸ Stamp Library… (Ctrl+Shift+L) | Pieces of map kept across maps |\n| [Terrain from Image](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-image-to-terrain) | On | File ▸ Import ▸ Terrain from Image… | Turns a picture into terrain |\n| [TrigScript](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-trigscript) | On | Triggers ▸ TrigScript… | Triggers as code; see [TrigScript](#trigscript) |\n| [Walkability](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-walkability) | On | View ▸ Walkability (Ctrl+Shift+W) | The ground as a unit walks it |\n| [TrigEdit](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-trigedit) | Off | Triggers ▸ Text Trigger Editor… (Ctrl+Shift+T) | Triggers as SCMDraft text |\n| [Magenta](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-magenta) | Browse | Triggers ▸ Magenta… | Triggers as sentences, with Remastered EUD |\n| [Melee Wizard](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-melee-wizard) | Browse | Tools ▸ Melee Wizard… (Ctrl+Shift+M) | Start locations and mineral lines |\n| [Mod Editor](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-mods) | Browse | File ▸ Open a Mod… | A mod's tables, strings, pictures and scripts, with the map drawn from them; see [Mods](mods.md) |\n| [Section Explorer](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-section-explorer) | Browse | Tools ▸ Section Explorer… (Ctrl+Shift+H) | The map file, section by section and byte by byte |\n| [Timelapse](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-timelapse) | Browse | View ▸ Timelapse… | Records the map as you build it |\n| [Trigger Map](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-trigger-map) | Browse | Triggers ▸ Trigger Map… | The triggers as a graph |\n\n*On* plugins are installed and running; *Off* is installed but needs ticking on in Plugins\n▸ Manage Plugins…; *Browse* is installed from Plugins ▸ Browse Plugins….\n\n![The Walkability overlay on Big Game Hunters](images/walkability.webp)\n\nA few that need more than a line:\n\n- **Paint** lays down whatever the active layer's palette has picked, so it paints units,\n  doodads, sprites, terrain or fog depending on the layer.\n- **Stamp Library** keeps named pieces of map, such as a ramp, a bridge, a cliff corner or\n  a mineral line, in the browser's storage. Click one and it hangs under the pointer,\n  drawn with the map's graphics; click to lay it down. Stamps can be searched, tagged,\n  exported and shared as a line of text.\n- **Melee Wizard** lays out symmetric start locations, and mineral lines and geysers at\n  the distance the game mines fastest from, with presets for main, natural and third, a\n  symmetry check and a resource summary.\n- **Timelapse** records one frame per change and plays the recording back with a box\n  around each change. Export it as a GIF or a WebM video. Opening the same file again\n  carries on the same recording.\n- **scm-sim** is the game engine behind the play page. Besides Test in Browser and Debug\n  Triggers in Browser, it is what Aftermath and Walkability use to have the game play a\n  replay or walk a map. It works only in the editor at scmjs.dev.\n\n![The Paint panel, drawing a line of the terrain palette's pick](images/paint.webp)\n\nEach plugin's own README, linked in the table, has the details.\n\n### Adding and managing plugins\n\n![Browse Plugins](images/browse-plugins.webp)\n\n**Plugins ▸ Browse Plugins…** lists the plugins the project publishes; press Install on\none and it shows where the code comes from before adding it. The plugins listed there are\nreviewed before they are listed.\n\n**Plugins ▸ Manage Plugins…** lists what is installed, turns each on or off, and takes the\naddress of any other plugin: a GitHub repository, a link to its `plugin.json`, or\n`http://localhost:3000/` while you write one. A plugin that has settings keeps them on its\nown page under Edit ▸ Preferences ▸ Plugins.\n\nThere is no sandbox: a plugin has the same access as the editor itself, so only add\nplugins you trust. The Add screen shows the manifest, the repository and the addresses the\nplugin will fetch from.\n\nBrowse Plugins also lists two plugins for people writing their own: [Hello\nWorld](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-hello-world), an example with nothing in it but a\nTools menu item, and the [API\nPlayground](https://github.com/scm-js/plugin-api-playground), a code editor for trying the\nplugin API on the open map. Installing plugins, what they are allowed to do, and writing\none are covered in [docs/plugins.md](plugins.md).\n\n## Your scmjs.dev account\n\n[scmjs.dev](https://scmjs.dev) is the project's own service. An account there does two\nthings for the editor:\n\n- it keeps maps for you, with a history of revisions and links that give anyone a copy;\n- it lets you share the map you have open so others can edit it with you at the same time\n  ([Editing a map together](#editing-a-map-together)).\n\nNone of it is required. The editor works completely without an account, and it sends\nnothing to scmjs.dev until you use one of these features; there is no request at startup\nunless you are already signed in.\n\nThe `scmjs` command line can sign in too, and make a copy link or an edit link from a map\nfile on your computer ([Sharing from the terminal](cli.md#sharing-from-the-terminal)).\n\nAll of it lives in the scmjs.dev plugin, which is installed and on from the start. If you\nwould rather not see the Account menu at all, turn the plugin off in Plugins ▸ Manage\nPlugins….\n\n### Signing in\n\n![The Account dialog, signed in](images/account.webp)\n\n**Account ▸ Sign in to scmjs.dev…**, or the *Sign in to scmjs.dev* cell at the right end\nof the status bar, starts the sign-in. Sign in with Discord: the provider's own page opens\nin a small window, you approve it there, and the window closes itself. The editor never\nsees a password, only a display name and an id from the provider.\n\nSigned in, the same cell (or **Account ▸ Account…**) opens the Account dialog, which\nshows:\n\n- your name and the sign-in you used;\n- a *Status* list of what the account can use: map storage, shared editing, and the AI\n  features (on your own key; see [The AI](#the-ai));\n- how much of your map storage is in use;\n- the maps you are sharing (see [Your shared maps](#your-shared-maps)).\n\nIts buttons open your account page on scmjs.dev (to link another sign-in, or delete the\naccount and everything with it), open My Maps, and sign out. Under *Settings*, a tick\nhides the status-bar cell.\n\nscmjs.dev keeps the id and display name from your sign-in and the maps you store there.\nThe account page deletes all of it.\n\n### Keeping maps on scmjs.dev\n\n![My Maps: three maps on the account, one with two revisions](images/my-maps.webp)\n\n**Account ▸ My Maps…** (also File ▸ Open from scmjs.dev…) lists the maps on your account,\nnewest change first, each with a picture, its tileset, size and player count, and how many\nrevisions it has. The box above the list searches the names, tilesets, file names and\nnotes. The map you last opened or saved from here is picked when the list arrives, and its\nrevisions appear on the right: the number, the note you wrote, the file name, the size and\nwhen it was saved. Up and Down move through the list, and Enter opens the map.\n\n- **Open** puts the picked revision in the editor, asking first if the map you have open\n  has unsaved changes.\n- **Download** saves the file to disk as it was uploaded.\n- The note can be added or edited later, and the map renamed.\n- A revision can be deleted, except the last one; to remove that, delete the map.\n\n![Save to scmjs.dev: a second revision of Big Game Hunters with a note](images/save-to-scmjs.webp)\n\n**Account ▸ Save to scmjs.dev…** (also under File, beside Save Copy As) saves the open map\nto the account, as a new map or as the next revision of one you pick; the map it was\nopened from or last saved to is picked for you. Write a note saying what changed, and tick\nwhether to include a one-pixel-per-tile picture for the list. The file is what File ▸ Save\nwould write, with the save options you last confirmed, so what comes back is playable. It\nis a copy: Ctrl+S goes on writing to the file on your disk, and nothing on the account\nchanges until you save there again.\n\nRevisions are numbered in order and a number is never reused. Saving a file whose bytes\nare already on the account costs no storage; only the new note is kept. Storage is 250 MB\nper account, shown as a bar in both dialogs.\n\n### Giving someone a copy\n\nA link can hand a map to anyone: whoever opens it gets a copy of the map in their own\neditor, without signing in. The copy is theirs: it opens as a new file, File ▸ Save asks\nwhere to keep it, and nothing they change reaches your map. To edit a map together\ninstead, see [Editing a map together](#editing-a-map-together).\n\n**Account ▸ Copy Link to This Map…** saves the open map to your account (as the next\nrevision of the map it came from, or as a new map) and gives you the link, already on the\nclipboard. The link opens the version you just saved, so a link you have posted somewhere\ndoes not change under the people who follow it; tick *Let the link follow my later saves*\nfor one that opens whatever you save to that map next.\n\nPasted into Discord, Slack, a forum or most other places that show previews, a link to the\nweb editor shows a card with the map's name, who shared it, its tileset, size and players,\nand its picture. A link that has been removed shows a card saying it no longer works.\n\n**Embed…** beside a link in My Maps gives you that card as a picture to put somewhere\nyourself, with the link around it: a forum post or signature (BBCode), a GitHub README or\na Discourse forum (Markdown), or a website (HTML). Pick the large picture (1200 × 630) or\nthe small one (600 × 315) for a signature, and copy the code. The picture's own address\nopens nothing: only the link you wrap it in does. When the link is removed, the picture\nsays the map is no longer shared.\n\nThe picture of the map on the card is drawn by your editor when it saves the map to\nscmjs.dev, so a map saved without the game's graphics installed shows its size instead.\n\nIn **My Maps**, the *Links* part of a map lists its links, how many times each was opened,\nand **Remove** for each; a removed link stops working at once and the map stays. *Link to\n#3* makes a link to the revision you are looking at, and *Link to the newest* makes one\nthat follows your saves. Deleting a revision removes the links to it, and deleting a map\nremoves all of its links. A map can have ten links.\n\nOpening a link starts the editor with an *Open a Copy* dialog showing the map's name,\npicture, size and who shared it; **Open a copy** opens it. **Account ▸ Open a Map Link…**\ntakes a link pasted in, which is the way in the desktop app.\n\n## Editing a map together\n\n![Big Game Hunters shared by three people: Kim's pointer beside the marines she just placed, Sam in Player Settings, and the edges of what each of them can see](images/share-editing.webp)\n\nSeveral people can work on one map at the same time, each in their own editor. One of them\nshares the map they have open and sends the others a link; whoever opens it gets the map\nin their editor, and from then on every change anyone makes shows up for everyone:\nterrain, doodads, units, sprites, locations, fog, the settings dialogs, triggers, strings\nand sounds. Each person also sees the others' pointers on the map with their names, a\ndashed box in their colour around what each of them has on screen, and which dialog they\nare in.\n\nSharing goes through scmjs.dev: the person who shares needs a signed-in\n[account](#your-scmjsdev-account), and the people joining need only the link.\n\n### Sharing a map\n\n![The Share dialog while the map is shared: the link, how long it is kept open, the three people in it, and Leave and End sharing](images/share-dialog.webp)\n\n**Account ▸ Share this Map…** asks for a name for the map (its own name to start with) and\nhow long to **keep it open**, and copies it to scmjs.dev when you press **Start sharing**.\nThe dialog then shows the link: **Copy** puts it on the clipboard for you to send. A cell\nsuch as *Shared · 3 people* appears in the status bar, and clicking it opens the dialog\nagain. Pasted into Discord, Slack or a forum, the link shows a card with the map's name,\nwho shared it and, for a map kept open, its picture.\n\n![Share this Map before sharing starts: the name, Keep it open set to For a week, and what that means](images/share-keep.webp)\n\n*Keep it open* decides what happens when people leave:\n\n- **Until everyone leaves** (the choice to start with) shares the map only while people\n  are in it. Nothing is stored: sharing ends when you stop it, half an hour after the last\n  person leaves, or when the server restarts.\n- **For a day**, **for a week** or **for a month** saves the map to [My\n  Maps](#keeping-maps-on-scmjsdev) and keeps it open at its link, so people can come and\n  go as they like, on different days if they want. Each time everyone has left, the map is\n  saved as a new revision with a note naming who changed it (*Edited together: Kim, Sam*),\n  and the next person to open the link carries on from there. The time counts from the\n  last edit, so a map people are working on stays open, and one nobody has touched for\n  that long ends. The dialog shows the date: *ends 3 Oct unless someone edits it*.\n- **Until I end it** keeps it open with no time limit. It still ends after a year with no\n  edits.\n\nWhen a map kept open ends, only the sharing ends: the map and its revisions stay in My\nMaps. If the map in front came from My Maps, sharing it adds a revision to that map rather\nthan making a second one.\n\nThe dialog lists who is in, in the colour their pointer is drawn in, and what each of them\nhas open. The link is the key: anyone who has it can join and edit, so send it only to\npeople you want in the map. Only you, as the person who shared it, see the link and the\ncontrols:\n\n- **Remove** sends one person out of the map. They keep what they had.\n- **New link** makes the old link stop working without sending anyone out, for when the\n  link went further than you meant.\n- **Stop sharing** (**End sharing** on a map kept open) ends it for everyone. On a map\n  kept open, **Leave** takes only you out and leaves it open for the others; you can\n  change how long it stays open here too.\n\nUp to eight people can be in one map, and an account can share five maps at a time,\ncounting both kinds. A map shared from the desktop app gets a link to the web editor,\nsince that is what anyone can open.\n\n### Your shared maps\n\n![The Shared maps part of the Account dialog: two maps kept open and one shared until everyone leaves, with who is in each, the last edit and when it ends](images/share-account.webp)\n\nThe **Shared maps** part of **Account ▸ Account…** lists every map you are sharing: who is\nin it now, the last edit and who made it, and when it ends. Each has **Join**, **Copy\nlink**, **New link**, how long to keep it open, and **End sharing**. When you are already\nsharing five, Share this Map… shows the same list so you can end one first.\n\n![My Maps with a map kept open: marked Shared · 3 editing, with Put #1 into the shared map and End sharing](images/share-mymaps.webp)\n\nA map kept open also has **Embed…** in that list and in the Share dialog. Its picture can\nopen a copy of the map (the default; a link to the newest revision is made for it) or the\nshared map itself. The second lets anyone who sees the picture join and edit, and the\ndialog says so; **New link** is the way back if it went further than you meant.\n\nIn **My Maps** a map kept open says so (*Shared · 2 editing*, or when it ends), and\n**Join** opens it with whoever is there; **Open** still gives you a revision on its own.\nWhile you are in one of your maps kept open, a revision of it has **Put #N into the shared\nmap**, which replaces the map for everyone with that revision: the way back if someone\nspoiled it. Everyone's undo history starts again, as after a resize on a shared map, and\nthe map as it was is not kept unless you saved it first.\n\n### Joining\n\n![Joining from a link: the map's name, who shared it, and the name the others will see](images/share-join.webp)\n\nOpen the link in a browser and the editor starts with the Join dialog up. In the desktop\napp, or in an editor that is already open, paste the link into **Account ▸ Join a Shared\nMap…**. The dialog says which map the link leads to, who shared it and how many people are\nin it; type the name the others will see you by and press **Join**. The map opens in a new\ntab beside whatever you had open (or in place of the empty map the editor starts with).\nClosing that tab leaves the shared map.\n\nYou can be in one shared map at a time. Joining another leaves the first, which stays open\nas an ordinary map.\n\n### Talking to each other\n\nWhile a shared map is in front, a **Chat** button sits in the bottom-right corner of the\nmap, next to the tileset and zoom. It opens a small window over the map with what everyone\nhas written and a line to type in; Enter sends. What you type there goes to everyone in\nthe map and nowhere else, and typing in it does not trigger the editor's shortcut keys.\n\nWhen the chat is closed, a new message shows as a notice and as a count on the button.\nAnyone who joins later sees what was said before them. The chat is kept only while people\nare in the map: it is gone when sharing ends, or when everyone has left a map kept open,\nand it is not saved with the map.\n\n### When two people change the same thing\n\n- **Your stroke is never cut in two.** While you hold the mouse button on the map, other\n  people's changes wait and arrive when you let go. The same happens while a dialog that\n  edits the map is open (Player Settings, the Trigger Editor, Unit Properties and the\n  like); the others see that you are in it, next to your pointer and in the Share dialog.\n- **The later change wins.** If two people paint the same tile, the change the server got\n  second stays. If two people move the same unit, the first move stands and the second is\n  dropped, because the unit is no longer where the second person's editor had it; the\n  status bar says when some of your change no longer applied. A dialog's OK writes its\n  whole table, so if two people change the triggers at the same time, the second OK wins.\n  When you see someone in the Trigger Editor, talk before you open it too.\n- **Your replaced version is kept.** If someone else's OK replaces triggers, a briefing or\n  settings you wrote, and they had not seen your version, a notice says so and stays until\n  you close it. **Put mine back** writes your version again, as one more undo step that\n  everyone receives; closing the notice keeps theirs. This works when everyone in the\n  session has an editor recent enough to say what each change was made from.\n- **Undo is yours.** Ctrl+Z takes back your own changes, not other people's, and leaves a\n  tile alone once someone else has painted over it. Undoing your own OK puts a table back\n  only if nobody has written it since.\n- **Resize, a tileset change and a raw section edit** replace the whole map for everyone,\n  and everyone's undo history starts again. On a map you edit alone, a resize or a tileset\n  change is an ordinary undo step instead.\n\n### Saving, and when it ends\n\nSaving is each person's own: File ▸ Save writes your copy to your disk, and anyone in the\nmap can save at any time. A map shared *until everyone leaves* is kept on scmjs.dev only\nwhile it is shared, and nothing of it is stored there afterwards; a map kept open is one\nof the sharer's maps in My Maps, and its revisions stay there after sharing ends. Everyone\nstill has the map open in their editor when sharing ends, so nobody loses work as long as\nthey save it.\n\nA server restart ends a map shared until everyone leaves, but not a map kept open: the\neditors in it reconnect on their own when the server is back and carry on.\n\nIf your connection drops, the status bar says *Reconnecting…* and you can keep working:\nyour changes are kept and sent once the connection is back, and you get everyone else's\nchanges from while you were gone. The others see you as having lost the connection\nmeanwhile. If you are away so long that the server has moved on, the shared map opens\nagain in a new tab with everyone's latest, and your copy stays open in its own tab, with a\nnote saying how many of your changes the others never got. After two minutes of trying,\nthe editor gives up and leaves the shared map; the map stays open, and the link still\nworks.\n\nIf the server cannot take one of your changes, your editor leaves the shared map and says\nso. The map stays open; save it, or open the link again to carry on with everyone's\nlatest.\n\n## The AI\n\n![The Tools ▸ AI menu](images/ai-menu.webp)\n\n**Tools ▸ AI** holds an assistant that reads and edits the open map with you, and tools\nthat lay out maps, write triggers, names and briefings, review a map and rewrite its\nstrings. They come with the scmjs.dev plugin and run on your own Anthropic API key.\n\n### Adding your key\n\n1. Make an API key at [console.anthropic.com](https://console.anthropic.com).\n2. Open **Edit ▸ Preferences ▸ Plugins ▸ scmjs.dev** (or *Add your key…* in the Account\n   dialog), paste the key under **Your Anthropic key** and press **Check and use**.\n3. The key is checked with Anthropic first, which costs nothing. When it passes, the\n   **Tools ▸ AI** menu, the assistant and the AI buttons in the editor's dialogs appear.\n\nNo scmjs.dev account is needed for this. Anthropic bills each request to your Anthropic\naccount. The key stays in your browser: scmjs.dev passes it on with each AI request and\nkeeps no copy. **Forget the key** on the same page removes it, and the AI features with\nit.\n\nA few things are the same for every feature:\n\n- **What is sent.** Only what the feature needs: what you typed and the map's facts (size,\n  tileset, players, what is where), and for some features a picture of the map, the\n  strings or the triggers. The map file itself is never sent.\n- **Undo.** Every change the AI makes is one undo step, labelled \"AI: …\", including what\n  it writes to the name and description, strings, triggers, players and unit settings.\n- **Cost.** Every AI dialog shows its progress while you wait, and what the request cost\n  when it finishes.\n\n### Where your key goes\n\nEach AI request goes from your browser to the scmjs.dev server with your key attached. The\nserver passes it to Anthropic and sends the answer back. It keeps nothing of the request:\nnot what you typed, not the answer, not your key or your address. To hold everyone to 30\nrequests a minute, 1,000 a day and 4 at once, it counts requests per address, in memory\nonly: the counts are never written to disk. What Anthropic keeps is set by Anthropic's own\nterms for the API.\n\nWhat the model is told, and what the editor does with its answer, is public in\n[scm-js/ai-engine](https://github.com/scm-js/ai-engine). Each AI feature is one file\nthere. The server's sign-in, accounts and limits are not public.\n\nIf a feature gives you a bad result, open an issue on\n[ai-engine](https://github.com/scm-js/ai-engine/issues) with what you asked, what came\nback, and the map if you can share it.\n\n### The assistant\n\n![The assistant placing a squad for Player 1 on Big Game Hunters](images/assistant.webp)\n\n**Assistant** (Ctrl+Shift+A, or the *AI* cell in the status bar) opens a conversation\nabout the open map. Ask a question or say what to change, and the assistant reads the map\nand edits it with the editor's own tools while you watch.\n\n- **What it reads:** everything, including units and their properties, doodads, sprites,\n  locations, strings, switches, sounds, triggers and the trigger script, unit, upgrade and\n  technology settings, fog, terrain, Check Map results, your selection, and a screenshot\n  of any area.\n- **What it changes:** nearly everything the editor can, from terrain and units to\n  triggers, players and forces, settings and the map's size. It draws terrain as shapes,\n  such as a plateau with a ramp that fits, a lane that stays walkable or a river with a\n  bridge, and can tell you whether units can walk from one place to another.\n\nEach turn is folded into one block under your message. While it runs, the block shows the\nlatest steps (**▸** a read, **✎** a change), and the map outlines what is about to change\nand follows the work, zooming out but never in. Scroll or zoom yourself and the view stays\nwhere you put it for the rest of the turn. When the turn ends, the block shows the steps,\nthe time and the cost, with an **Undo** button that reverses the whole turn. Escape stops\na turn.\n\nTo start a message, use the suggestion chips above the input, or right-click the map and\nchoose *Ask AI about this spot / the selection / this area…*. Tick *Picture* to send a\nscreenshot of the visible area. Player Settings has a *Set up with AI…* button that sets\nup players and forces from one sentence. After 24 rounds of tool calls the assistant stops\nand offers to continue.\n\n### Make Scenario\n\n![Make Scenario: the design document for a four-player madness map](images/make-scenario.webp)\n\n**Make Scenario…** builds a whole scenario from a sentence, such as \"a madness map\" or \"a\ntwo-lane tower defense\". Set the size, tileset and number of players and press **Design**.\n\n1. **The design.** The model writes a design document first: the premise, the players and\n   forces, each trigger system and its settings, the layout and its locations, the\n   objectives and the briefing. Edit it directly, or describe what should change and press\n   **Design again**. A location name can include `{p}`, as in \"Spawn {p}\", to build that\n   system once per player.\n2. **The build.** **Build** works through the design one step at a time, each step a row\n   that passes or fails on its own: the terrain and locations, players and forces, each\n   system, the objectives and briefing, the name, and a Check Map at the end. Common\n   layouts (corner camps, lanes, arenas, a bound's course, a town) are laid out by the\n   editor itself; others are drawn from the model's shapes. Common systems (spawns,\n   income, waves, lives, shops, leaderboards and the like) are built by the editor and\n   shown in green; anything else is written in [TrigScript](#trigscript) and shown in\n   gold.\n\nWhen it finishes, **Review it…** or **Open the assistant** to keep going.\n\n### Generate Map and Redo Area\n\n![Generate Map: the plan as a coloured grid, with the designer's notes](images/generate-map.webp)\n\n**Generate Map…** lays out a melee map from a description: the number of players, the kind\nof terrain, where the bases go and the symmetry. It shows a *plan* first: a rough grid of\nterrain, the bases with their resources, ramps, a name and the designer's notes. **Apply**\npaints it with the isometric brush onto a new map, or onto the open map if it is the same\nsize. **Refine** sends the plan back with your changes (\"more room around the naturals\")\nand a picture of the result. Treat the result as a starting point, and check the ramps\nagainst the cliffs before you play.\n\n**Redo Area…** does the same for part of an existing map. Mark an area (or right-click it\nand choose *Redo this area with AI…*) and describe what should be there; the new ground\njoins the old at the edges.\n\n### Triggers, names and strings\n\n- **Write Triggers…** turns a description into [TrigScript](#trigscript) and applies it,\n  keeping the script with the map. It can add to the map's triggers or replace them.\n- **Explain Triggers…** describes what the triggers do in play: all of them, a range, the\n  mission briefing, or an answer to a question. The Trigger Editor and Mission Briefing\n  Editor have the same *Explain*, *Write…* and *Ask* buttons.\n- **Name and Describe…** suggests three names with descriptions from what is on the map.\n  Map Properties has the same as a *Suggest a name* button.\n- **Write Briefing…** writes objectives and narration and creates a mission briefing for\n  each player.\n- **Review Map…** critiques the map from a picture, its statistics and Check Map, with a\n  **Go to** button on each finding that has a place.\n- **Rewrite Strings…** translates, corrects or shortens the map's strings, showing a\n  before-and-after table to tick. It is also the String Editor's *Rewrite with AI…*\n  button.\n\n![Review Map on Big Game Hunters](images/review-map.webp)\n\n### Options\n\n**Tools ▸ AI ▸ Options…** opens the same Preferences page as the key:\n\n- **Quality**: *Quick*, *Standard* (each feature at the level it was tuned for) or\n  *Thorough*. Higher quality takes longer and costs more.\n- **Spending**: a ceiling for one assistant message and for one Make Scenario run. At the\n  ceiling the work stops with the map as edited so far. 0 means no ceiling.\n- **Assistant**: rounds per message, the picture tick, following the work on the map,\n  docking the panel on the right, and showing the model's reasoning summary.\n- **Use the AI features**: unticked, it removes the menu, the assistant, the status-bar\n  cell and the AI buttons, and keeps your key.\n\n## Keyboard and preferences\n\nPress F1 for a list of every shortcut. These are the ones the editor starts with; any of\nthe commands can be given other keys on Preferences ▸ Hotkeys:\n\n| Keys | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `T` `D` `U` `S` `L` `F` `C` | switch layer |\n| `[` `]` | brush size |\n| `M` | symmetry on / off |\n| Alt+click | pick what is under the cursor |\n| Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y | undo / redo |\n| Del / Esc | delete selection / stop placing |\n| Ctrl+O, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+N | open, save, new |\n| Ctrl+Shift+S | save as |\n| Ctrl+T | Trigger Editor |\n| Ctrl+Shift+T | Text Trigger Editor (when TrigEdit is on) |\n| Ctrl+Shift+A | AI assistant (with your Anthropic key) |\n| Ctrl+F | find |\n| Ctrl+A | select all on the layer |\n| Ctrl+G | grid |\n| Ctrl+0, Ctrl+Shift+0 | 100%, zoom to fit |\n| Alt+Enter | map properties |\n| Ctrl+F5 | test map |\n| Ctrl+, | preferences |\n| Ctrl+Tab, Ctrl+Shift+Tab | next / previous open map (desktop app) |\n| Ctrl+W | close map (desktop app) |\n\n![Preferences](images/preferences.webp)\n\nPreferences (Ctrl+,) are kept in the browser. Nothing is written until OK or Apply, and\n**Reset to defaults** puts every page back. The pages down the left:\n\n**General**\n\n- **Language**: English or Korean. The default follows the browser's, and a change applies\n  at once. A map's own text is never translated.\n- **Startup**: the splash screen; whether to reopen the last map (the desktop app does it\n  straight away; a browser that has to ask before reading the file again offers it in a\n  notice); how many recent files to keep. In the desktop app, whether to check for updates\n  at startup, and whether to include nightly builds.\n- **Maps**: whether each map opens in its own tab, and whether to ask before closing or\n  replacing a map with unsaved changes.\n- **New scenario**: the tileset, size and revision a new map starts with.\n- **Saving**: what the Save dialog starts from (how the file was opened, *Everything* or\n  *Smallest*), the compression for a map with no file yet, and whether a save keeps the\n  file it replaces (a `.bak`, or a previous version in a browser).\n- **Recovery**: whether to keep [recovery copies](#recovery-copies) of unsaved maps, and\n  how often.\n- **Privacy** (the editor at scmjs.dev only): whether to count which parts of the editor\n  are used. It is on unless you turn it off. When you use a feature, the editor sends its\n  name and a few words about it, such as which dialog opened, which layer an edit was on,\n  the type of each unit placed, or the tileset and size of a map opened. When something\n  goes wrong, it sends where in the editor's code the error happened (the file, line and\n  function) and whether the browser was translating the page, but never the error's\n  message. It never sends the map itself, its name, a file name, its text or anything you\n  typed, and nothing that identifies you. Turning it off also stops the visit count for\n  this browser, and the site's [privacy page](https://scmjs.dev/privacy.html) has the same\n  switch and the details. The desktop app and copies of the editor hosted elsewhere count\n  nothing.\n\n**Editing**: the grid's spacing, colour and style, and what snaps to it (View ▸ Grid\nSettings… opens this page); what the palettes start on (the owner of placed units, the\nbrush size, the size of a new location); and how many undo levels each map keeps.\n\n**View**: whether the mouse wheel scrolls (Ctrl+wheel zooms) or zooms, and whether a wheel\nzoom keeps the tile under the pointer in place; whether water and units animate and how\nfast; whether the map starts out drawn with Remastered's graphics; which cells the status\nbar shows; and whether string previews follow Remastered's colour rule or 1.16.1's.\n\n**Testing**: where Test Map sends the map (at scmjs.dev, StarCraft or the browser), the\nfolder it writes into for StarCraft, in the desktop app whether the game starts after the\nmap is written, and how the browser plays the map.\n\n**Plugins**: what to do when an installed plugin has a newer version (tell you, do\nnothing, or install it; see [docs/plugins.md](plugins.md#keeping-a-plugin-up-to-date)),\nand each plugin's own settings page.\n\n**Storage**: where the game data comes from, and everything the editor keeps in the\nbrowser, one row per setting or cache, with a plugin's own data under its name. Each row\ncan be cleared on its own, or **Clear all data** throws the lot away. Maps are not part of\nthat list and are not touched by it. The recovery copies and the previous versions of\nsaved files have their own lines. **Export** writes the settings, the plugins' included,\nto one file, and **Import** reads such a file into another browser or machine; caches and\nthe recent files stay behind.\n\n**Hotkeys**: every command and its keys.\n\n- **+** on a row waits for the next keys you press and adds them (Esc stops waiting); the\n  × on a key removes it. A command can have several keys or none.\n- Keys used by two commands, or by a command and a plugin, are marked. A plugin's keys are\n  tried first; between two commands, the one higher in the list wins.\n- Delete, Esc, the arrows, Tab, Enter, Space and F11 keep their own jobs and cannot be\n  given to a command.\n- A plain key such as `T` never fires while you type in a text box, and most Ctrl\n  shortcuts leave a text box's own undo, clipboard and select-all alone.\n- The browser keeps Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+W for its own tabs, so Next Map and Close Map start\n  with no keys there; give them others if you want them.\n\nThe placement options, the panels and the recent files are remembered too.\n\n### What's new\n\n**Help ▸ What's New…** lists what changed in each release, newest first, with the release\nyou are running open and the older ones folded below it. The part written for plugin\nauthors is folded as well. The notes come with the editor, so the list works offline and\nalways matches the version in front of you; a nightly build shows the notes of the last\nrelease. The version line in **Help ▸ About scmJS…** opens the same list.\n\nThe first time you start a release with new notes, a notice says the editor has been\nupdated and offers the list. It appears once per release. The notes are written in\nEnglish.\n\nIn the desktop app, **Help ▸ Check for Updates…** looks for a newer version.\n\n## When something goes wrong\n\nIf the editor does something strange, the debug console shows what happened, **Copy Bug\nReport** puts it on the clipboard, and **Report an Issue** sends it to the project.\n\n### Damaged and protected maps\n\nThe Repair plugin checks every map as it opens, the way the game reads it. Protected maps\nin particular often have parts missing, damaged or repeated.\n\n- **Safe repairs are made at once**: the ones that only write what the game already does,\n  or put back what the editor needs, such as the isometric record a protector stripped.\n  They do not mark the map as changed, and the next save writes them.\n- **Judgement calls are left to you**, such as a player slot with a race the game does not\n  have. A cell in the status bar (*Repaired 3 · 1 to review*) opens a list that says what\n  the game does about each one and what the repair would change.\n\nUndo in that list puts the file back as it opened. Tools ▸ Repair Map… runs the check\nagain, and Edit ▸ Preferences ▸ Plugins ▸ Repair can make it ask before repairing\nanything, or not check at all. To look at the file section by section and byte by byte,\ninstall Section Explorer from Plugins ▸ Browse Plugins…. [Protected and damaged\nmaps](file-formats.md#protected-and-damaged-maps) has the details.\n\n### The debug console\n\n**View ▸ Debug Console** opens a strip along the bottom of the window with the log in it:\nmaps opened and saved, plugins started and stopped, where the game data came from, and\nevery error the page threw.\n\n![The debug console](images/debug-console.webp)\n\nThe log is kept whether the strip is open or not, so opening it *after* something odd\nhappened still shows what happened. The last couple of thousand lines are kept; **Clear**\nempties it, for starting clean before reproducing something.\n\n**Warnings** and **Errors** narrow the list to what went wrong; the box beside them\nfilters on any text. **Verbose** adds a line for every edit and every call a plugin makes\ninto the editor. It answers \"which plugin did that?\", it is noisy by design, and it turns\nitself off when you next load the editor.\n\n### When part of the editor fails\n\nIf a panel, the palette, the map view or the menu bar hits an error it cannot draw past,\nthat part alone is replaced by a short notice with the error in it. Everything else keeps\nworking, your maps included. **Try again** draws the part afresh, and **Show the log**\nopens the debug console at the line that says what failed.\n\nA dialog that fails is closed, and a notice says so with the same **Show the log** button.\nWhat you had changed in it and not yet applied is lost; what you had applied is in the\nmap.\n\nIf the whole editor fails, a plain page takes its place with the error, the end of the\nlog, and **Copy the log** and **Save the log…** for a bug report. Your maps are still open\nbehind it: **Try again** brings the editor back with them. A recovery copy of each map\nwith unsaved changes is written as that page appears (unless you switched recovery copies\noff in Preferences), so **Reload** offers them back too.\n\n### Copying a bug report\n\n**Help ▸ Copy Bug Report** puts the log on the clipboard with a short header above it (the\nversion, the browser or desktop app, where the game data came from, the map's size and\ntileset, and the plugins with their versions), ready to paste into an issue, a forum post\nor a message. A long session is trimmed to its most recent lines so the paste fits in an\nissue; when it is, the report says so.\n\nThe console's own **Copy** does the same without the trim, and **Save…** writes the whole\nlog to a file, for when it is too long to paste.\n\n### Filing an issue\n\n**Help ▸ Report an Issue…** opens a new issue on GitHub with the report already filled in,\nunder three questions: what you did, what you expected, and what happened. Answer those,\ncheck the rest, and submit. You need a GitHub account.\n\nThe issue holds a shortened log. If the lines that matter were cut, use **Copy Bug\nReport** and paste the full report over it.\n\nNothing is sent until you submit the issue yourself. The report names the map *file* you\nhave open, the plugins you have installed and the version you are running, and nothing\nelse about you.\n\n## What it does not do\n\nThis section lists what the editor does not do, and the limits worth knowing before you\nrely on a feature.\n\n### Undo and history\n\n- Undo takes back what a dialog's OK or Apply wrote, as one step. What you change inside\n  an open dialog before that is not in the history; Cancel is the way back. The larger\n  editors (triggers, the briefing, strings, sounds, switches, and the unit, upgrade and\n  technology settings) ask before Escape, the close button or a click outside the dialog\n  throws away changes you have not applied.\n- A raw section edit (a plugin rewriting the file's sections, such as an edit in Section\n  Explorer) clears the undo history. So do a resize and a tileset change on a shared map;\n  on a map you edit alone each is one undo step.\n- A step that rewrites thousands of triggers (a TrigScript build, say) is kept, but the\n  oldest steps are then dropped sooner than the undo-levels setting says, so the history\n  does not grow without bound.\n\n### Browsers and the desktop app\n\n- In Firefox and Safari, every save is a download, and a recent map reopens through File ▸\n  Open instead of straight from disk. Chrome, Edge and the desktop app write in place.\n- In a browser, Tools ▸ Test in StarCraft writes the map where the game lists it but\n  cannot start the game; in Firefox and Safari it downloads the map. At scmjs.dev, Test\n  Map plays it in a browser tab unless Preferences ▸ Testing says StarCraft.\n- The Windows zip does not register `.scm`, `.scx` and `.chk`, since nothing is installed.\n  A map dragged onto `scmJS.exe` still opens.\n- On macOS the app sees a new version but cannot install it until the app is code-signed,\n  so it offers the download page instead.\n\n### Terrain and objects\n\n- The isometric brush needs the map's isometric record. The Repair plugin, on by default,\n  rebuilds one a protected map had stripped.\n- The isometric brush paints the game's terrain types only. Flat ground a mod adds is laid\n  with the Rect brush and its seams by hand ([Ground a mod adds](#ground-a-mod-adds)).\n- Symmetry does not mirror the Blend brush or pasting. The Tile brush copies the same tile\n  to each image, so an edge tile faces the same way on both sides; use the isometric brush\n  for cliffs and shores.\n- Changing the tileset lays the terrain again and drops the doodads; the units, sprites,\n  locations, fog and triggers stay.\n\n### Triggers and text\n\n- In the Trigger Editor, EUD values are raw numbers: the EPD box turns a memory address\n  into the player value that reaches it, but nothing names what an address holds. For\n  named reads and writes of the game, use a [TrigScript](trigscript.md#programs) program\n  or the Magenta plugin. Both play on Remastered only.\n- Transmission is the one briefing action no Blizzard map uses, so its layout could not be\n  checked against one.\n- TrigScript's programs play on Remastered only; `trigger()` works on every version. The\n  full list is under [TrigScript's reference](trigscript.md#reference).\n- A character the map's text encoding cannot hold is saved as `?`. Check Map and the Save\n  dialog say so beforehand.\n- The Korean translation was not written by a native speaker. A review is welcome (see\n  [docs/development.md](development.md#translations)).\n\n### The scmjs.dev features\n\n- Keeping maps on an account and editing a map together run on scmjs.dev and need a\n  connection. Keeping and sharing maps need an account; joining a shared map needs only\n  the link.\n- The AI features need your own Anthropic API key and a connection (see [The\n  AI](#the-ai)).\n";
